iabetes been lately?\n\n[patient]: It\'s been a bit of a challenge. My blood sugars have been fluctuating a lot.\n\n[doctor]: Hmm, that doesn\'t sound good. Can you tell me more about what\'s been going on?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I think it\'s because of the diet change. I\'ve been eating a lot more sweets since I started working at the bakery.\n\n[doctor]: Ah, that makes sense. It can be tough to resist those sweet treats when you\'re working in a bakery. Have you been taking your medication as prescribed?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I\'ve been taking my metformin twice a day and haven\'t missed any doses.\n\n[doctor]: Good job on that. Your blood pressure has been stable, right?\n\n[patient]: Yes, my blood pressure has been good.\n\n[doctor]: That\'s good to hear. How about your sleep? How many hours are you sleeping each night?\n\n[patient]: I\'ve been sleeping about 8 hours a night, which is good.\n\n[doctor]: Excellent. Do you ever have any thoughts of self-harm or suicidal ideations?\n\n[patient]: No, I haven\'t had any of those thoughts.\n\n[doctor]: Great. Now, let\'s do a quick physical exam. Can you please roll up your sleeve so I can check your blood pressure?\n\n[patient]: Sure.\n\n[doctor]: Great, thank you. Your blood pressure looks good. Your neck is supple and there\'s no swelling. Your lungs sound clear, and I don\'t hear any wheezing or rales. Your heart sounds regular, and there\'s a slight murmur, but it\'s not concerning. Your pulses are good.\n\n[patient]: Okay.\n\n[doctor]: Now, let\'s talk about your blood work. Your hemoglobin A1c was elevated recently.\n\n[patient]: Oh, I didn\'t know that. What does that mean?\n\n[doctor]: It means your blood glucose levels have been higher than they should be. We\'ll need to get them under control. You\'re currently taking metformin twice a day, but I think we need to make some adjustments to your diet.\n\n[patient]: Okay, I understand.\n\n[doctor]: I\'ll continue your metformin twice a day. I also want you to start c