-d '{'model': 'gpt-4o', 'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a fair judge assistant assigned to deliver insightful feedback that compares individual performances, highlighting how each stands relative to others within the same cohort.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of two responses strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, choose a better response between Response A and Response B. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: "(write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (A or B)"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###Instruction:\n\nImagine you are a medical professional tasked with evaluating simulated doctor-patient conversations generated from summary notes. \nThese conversations should accurately reconstruct interactions based on the SOAP (Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan) format provided in the notes.\n\nHere is the medical note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAnnual exam.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nBeverly Ortiz is a 24-year-old female patient with a history of type 2 diabetes and high blood pressure. She is here today for her yearly exam.\n\nThe patient states she has been doing well. She has recently began working at a bakery this summer.\n\nThe patient states her diabetes is uncontrolled. Her blood sugars have fluctuated recently, which she attributes to her change in diet due to work. She is trying to improve her diet by removing additional sweets. She has been taking her medication as prescribed and has not missed any doses.\n\nThe patient states her blood pressure has been good despite her blood sugar levels being up and down. She has continued taking metformin twice a day.\n\nShe has been sleeping well and is able to sleep a full 8 hours. She denies thoughts of self-harm or suicidal ideations.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• Neurological/Psychological: Grip strength equal bilaterally. Neurologically intact.\n• Neck: Supple without thyromegaly or lymphadenopathy. No carotid bruits appreciable.\nLungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n• Cardiovascular: Regular rate. Grade 2/6 systolic ejection fraction murmur. No gallops or rubs. No extra heart sounds. Good pulses.\n\nRESULTS\n\nHemoglobin A1c is elevated.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nBeverly Ortiz is a 24-year-old female patient with a history of type 2 diabetes and high blood pressure. She is here today for her yearly exam.\n\nDiabetes mellitus type 2.\n• Medical Reasoning: Her blood glucose levels have been uncontrolled. She is still taking metformin twice a day, but does admit to dietary indiscretion with an increase in her sweets intake.. Her most recent hemoglobin A1c was elevated.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue metformin twice a day.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged her to check her fasting blood glucose levels each morning and call them in about 2 weeks. She will follow up in about 2 months, at which time we will discuss further treatment recommendations.\n\nHypertension.\n• Medical Reasoning: Appears to be relatively stable.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue lisinopril 10 mg once daily.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor]: Good morning, how are you doing today?\n\n[patient]: Hi, I\'m doing well. Thanks for seeing me.\n\n[doctor]: Great to see you too, Beverly. How\'s your job at the bakery going?\n\n[patient]: It\'s going great. I just started working there this summer.\n\n[doctor]: That\'s wonderful. I\'m glad you\'re enjoying it. How\'s your diabetes been lately?\n\n[patient]: It\'s been a bit of a challenge. My blood sugars have been fluctuating a lot.\n\n[doctor]: Hmm, that doesn\'t sound good. Can you tell me more about what\'s been going on?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I think it\'s because of the diet change. I\'ve been eating a lot more sweets since I started working at the bakery.\n\n[doctor]: Ah, that makes sense. It can be tough to resist those sweet treats when you\'re working in a bakery. Have you been taking your medication as prescribed?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I\'ve been taking my metformin twice a day and haven\'t missed any doses.\n\n[doctor]: Good job on that. Your blood pressure has been stable, right?\n\n[patient]: Yes, my blood pressure has been good.\n\n[doctor]: That\'s good to hear. How about your sleep? How many hours are you sleeping each night?\n\n[patient]: I\'ve been sleeping about 8 hours a night, which is good.\n\n[doctor]: Excellent. Do you ever have any thoughts of self-harm or suicidal ideations?\n\n[patient]: No, I haven\'t had any of those thoughts.\n\n[doctor]: Great. Now, let\'s do a quick physical exam. Can you please roll up your sleeve so I can check your blood pressure?\n\n[patient]: Sure.\n\n[doctor]: Great, thank you. Your blood pressure looks good. Your neck is supple and there\'s no swelling. Your lungs sound clear, and I don\'t hear any wheezing or rales. Your heart sounds regular, and there\'s a slight murmur, but it\'s not concerning. Your pulses are good.\n\n[patient]: Okay.\n\n[doctor]: Now, let\'s talk about your blood work. Your hemoglobin A1c was elevated recently.\n\n[patient]: Oh, I didn\'t know that. What does that mean?\n\n[doctor]: It means your blood glucose levels have been higher than they should be. We\'ll need to get them under control. You\'re currently taking metformin twice a day, but I think we need to make some adjustments to your diet.\n\n[patient]: Okay, I understand.\n\n[doctor]: I\'ll continue your metformin twice a day. I also want you to start checking your fasting blood glucose levels each morning. We\'ll need to monitor these levels closely to see how they change.\n\n[patient]: Alright, I\'ll do that.\n\n[doctor]: Great. Your blood pressure has been stable, but we\'ll keep an eye on that as well. You\'re currently taking lisinopril 10 mg once daily, and that seems to be working well.\n\n[patient]: Yes, I\'ve been taking it as prescribed.\n\n[doctor]: Good. We\'ll continue that. I\'ll see you in about 2 months, at which time we\'ll discuss further treatment recommendations based on your blood glucose levels.\n\n[patient]: Okay, that sounds good.\n\n[doctor]: Do you have any questions or concerns?\n\n[patient]: No, I think I understand everything.\n\n[doctor]: Alright, I\'ll see you in 2 months. Take care and feel free to call if you have any questions or concerns before then.\n\n[patient]: Thank you, I will. Have a good day.\n\n[doctor]: You too, take care. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Doctor: Hi. How are you today? \nPatient: I\'m good, thank you.\nDoctor: Good. Um, hi. Um, can you tell me a little bit about your history of illness? Um, can you start with your medical history?\nPatient: Um, okay. Um, so I\'m 24 years old. Um, I have type 2 diabetes and high blood pressure. Um, and I\'m here for my yearly exam.\nDoctor: Okay, okay. So, um, can you tell me a little bit more about your history of diabetes? Um, what\'s your history of diabetes like?\nPatient: Um, so, I was diagnosed about three years ago. Um, and I\'ve been taking metformin twice a day and my blood sugars have been pretty well controlled. Um, but recently, I\'ve been having a little bit of trouble with my blood sugars. They\'ve been fluctuating a bit.\nDoctor: Okay, okay. And what\'s been going on with your blood sugars? Um, what kind of fluctuations have you been experiencing?\nPatient: Um, so I\'ve been having a little bit of trouble with my blood sugars going up and down. Um, I\'ve been having a little bit of trouble sleeping, um, and just, you know, just feeling a little bit fatigued. Um, and I\'ve been having a little bit of trouble with my vision. Um, I\'ve been seeing a little bit of blurry vision.\nDoctor: Okay, okay. And what\'s been going on with your vision? Um, have you had any changes in your vision?\nPatient: Um, so I\'ve been having a little bit of trouble with my vision. Um, I\'ve been seeing a little bit of blurry vision. Um, but I\'ve been having a little bit of trouble with my blood sugar levels. Um, I\'ve been having a little bit of trouble with my blood sugar levels, um, and just, you know, just feeling a little bit fatigued.\nDoctor: Okay, okay. And how long has this been going on?\nPatient: Um, I\'d say about the past few months. Um, so I\'ve been having a little bit of trouble with my blood sugar levels, um, and just, you know, just feeling a little bit fatigued. Um, and I\'ve been having a little bit of trouble with my vision. Um, but I\'ve been having a little bit of trouble with my vision. Um, but I\'ve been having a little bit of trouble with my vision.\nDoctor: Okay, okay. And have you been taking your medications as prescribed?\nPatient: Um, yes. I\'ve been taking my medications as prescribed. Um, I\'ve been taking metformin twice a day. Um, and I\'ve been taking lisinopril 10 mg once daily.\nDoctor: Okay, okay. And um, have you had any changes in your diet or exercise recently?\nPatient: Um, yes. Um, so I\'ve recently started working at a bakery, and I\'ve been having a little bit of trouble with my diet. Um, I\'ve been having a little bit of trouble with my diet. Um, I\'ve been eating a little bit more sweets and I\'ve been having a little bit of trouble with my blood sugar levels.\nDoctor: Okay, okay. And how long have you been working at the bakery?\nPatient: Um, about a month now.\nDoctor: Okay, okay. And what kind of work do you do at the bakery?\nPatient: Um, so I work in the front, I\'m a cashier. Um, I\'m in charge of handling money and taking orders and making change. Um, so I\'m on my feet a lot and I\'m moving around a lot. Um, so I\'m not sitting down very much.\nDoctor: Okay, okay. And how\'s your sleep been?\nPatient: Um, so I\'ve been sleeping well. Um, I\'ve been sleeping about 8 hours a night.\nDoctor: Okay, okay. And have you been having any trouble with your mood?\nPatient: Um, no. I\'ve been feeling fine.\nDoctor: Okay, okay. And have you been having any trouble with your appetite?\nPatient: Um, no. I\'ve been eating fine. Um, I\'ve been eating a little bit more sweets, but I\'ve been eating fine.\nDoctor: Okay, okay. And have you been having any trouble with your bowels?\nPatient: Um, no. I\'ve been having regular bowel movements.\nDoctor: Okay, okay. And have you been having any trouble with your urination?\nPatient: Um, no. I\'ve been having normal urination.\nDoctor: Okay, okay. And have you been having any trouble with your skin?\nPatient: Um, no. I\'ve been having normal skin.\nDoctor: Okay, okay. And have you been having any trouble with your joints?\nPatient: Um, no. I\'ve been having normal joints.\nDoctor: Okay, okay. And have you been having any trouble with your neck?\nPatient: Um, no. I\'ve been having normal neck.\nDoctor: Okay, okay. And have you been having any trouble with your back?\nPatient: Um, no. I\'ve been having normal back.\nDoctor: Okay, okay. And have you been having any trouble with your abdomen?\nPatient: Um, no. I\'ve been having normal abdomen.\nDoctor: Okay, okay. And have you been having any trouble with your extremities?\nPatient: Um, no. I\'ve been having normal extremities.\nDoctor: Okay, okay. And have you been having any trouble with your eyes?\nPatient: Um, no. I\'ve been having normal eyes.\nDoctor: Okay, okay. And have you been having any trouble with your hearing?\nPatient: Um, no. I\'ve been having normal hearing.\nDoctor: Okay, okay. And have you been having any trouble with your vision?\nPatient: Um, no. I\'ve been having normal vision.\nDoctor: Okay, okay. And have you been having any trouble with your ears?\nPatient: Um, no. I\'ve been having normal ears.\nDoctor: Okay, okay. And have you been having any trouble with your nose?\nPatient: Um, no. I\'ve been having normal nose.\nDoctor: Okay, okay. And have you been having any trouble with your throat?\nPatient: Um, no. I\'ve been having normal throat.\nDoctor: Okay, okay. And have you been having any trouble with your mouth?\nPatient: Um, no. I\'ve been having normal mouth.\nDoctor: Okay, okay. And have you been having any trouble with your tongue?\nPatient: Um, no. I\'ve been having normal tongue.\nDoctor: Okay, okay. And have you been having any trouble with your lips?\nPatient: Um, no. I\'ve been having normal lips.\nDoctor: Okay, okay. And have you been having any trouble with your teeth?\nPatient: Um, no. I\'ve been having normal teeth.\nDoctor: Okay, okay. And have you been having any trouble with your gums?\nPatient: Um, no. I\'ve been having normal gums.\nDoctor: Okay, okay. And have you been having any trouble with your breath?\nPatient: Um, no. I\'ve been having normal breath.\nDoctor: Okay, okay. And have you been having any trouble with your heart?\nPatient: Um, no. I\'ve been having normal heart.\nDoctor: Okay, okay. And have you been having any trouble with your lungs?\nPatient: Um, no. I\'ve been having normal lungs.\nDoctor: Okay, okay. And have you been having any trouble with your abdomen?\nPatient: Um, no. I\'ve been having normal abdomen.\nDoctor: Okay, okay. And have you been having any trouble with your chest?\nPatient: Um, no. I\'ve been having normal chest.\nDoctor: Okay, okay. And have you been having any trouble with your back?\nPatient: Um, no. I\'ve been having normal back.\nDoctor: Okay, okay. And have you been having any trouble with your neck?\nPatient: Um, no. I\'ve been having normal neck.\nDoctor: Okay, okay. And have you been having any trouble with your head?\nPatient: Um, no. I\'ve been having normal head.\nDoctor: Okay, okay. And have you been having any trouble w