-d '{'model': 'gpt-4o', 'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a fair judge assistant assigned to deliver insightful feedback that compares individual performances, highlighting how each stands relative to others within the same cohort.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of two responses strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, choose a better response between Response A and Response B. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: "(write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (A or B)"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###Instruction:\n\nImagine you are a medical professional tasked with evaluating simulated doctor-patient conversations generated from summary notes. \nThese conversations should accurately reconstruct interactions based on the SOAP (Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan) format provided in the notes.\n\nHere is the medical note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nHigh blood pressure check.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMs. Susan Watson is a 26-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for a high blood pressure check.\n\nThe patient reports she has always struggled with high blood pressure, but it is seems out of control recently. She notes that she has interest in new medication or diet changes to help. She is currently on Norvasc 10 mg daily. Her blood pressure at home has been over 150. She notes she has had some headaches, but she is unsure if it is due to stress at work or her high blood pressure. She denies chest pain or shortness of breath.\n\nThe patient underwent a kidney transplant a few years ago, and everything is going well. She is taking all of her immunosuppression medication.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain or dyspnea on exertion.\n• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath\n• Neurological: Endorses headaches.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• Constitutional: in no apparent distress.\n• Neck: Supple without thyromegaly or lymphadenopathy.\n• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n• Cardiovascular: 2/6 systolic ejection murmur.\n• Musculoskeletal: No edema.\n• Integumentary: Scar on left flank from kidney transplant, healed.\n\nRESULTS\n\nCreatinine: normal.\n\nElectrocardiogram: normal.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nMs. Susan Watson is a 26-year-old female who is here today for a high blood pressure check.\n\nHypertension.\n• Medical Reasoning: Her blood pressure is elevated today and based on her recent home monitoring.\n• Additional Testing: I would like to get a lipid panel and metabolic panel.\n• Medical Treatment: carvedilol 25 mg twice a day to help bring her blood pressure down.\n\nStatus post kidney transplant.\n• Medical Reasoning: Doing well.\nKidney function stable.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue current medications.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor] Hi there, Susan. How are you today?\n\n[patient] Hi, I\'m doing alright. I\'m here for my high blood pressure check.\n\n[doctor] Great, let\'s go ahead and get started. So, you mentioned your blood pressure has been a bit out of control lately. Can you tell me more about that?\n\n[patient] Yes, it\'s been really high. I\'ve been checking it at home and it\'s been over 150.\n\n[doctor] Hmm, that\'s concerning. Have you noticed any other symptoms, like headaches or chest pain?\n\n[patient] Yes, I\'ve had some headaches, but I\'m not sure if it\'s from the high blood pressure or stress at work.\n\n[doctor] Okay, let\'s go through some other things. Have you had any chest pain or shortness of breath?\n\n[patient] No, no chest pain or shortness of breath.\n\n[doctor] Alright. You mentioned you\'ve had a kidney transplant a few years ago. How is everything going with that?\n\n[patient] It\'s been going well. I\'m taking all my immunosuppression medication as prescribed.\n\n[doctor] Great to hear that. Let\'s go through a quick review of your systems. How has your cardiovascular system been? Any chest pain or difficulty with exercise?\n\n[patient] No, no chest pain or shortness of breath on exertion.\n\n[doctor] And how about your respiratory system? Any shortness of breath?\n\n[patient] No, no shortness of breath.\n\n[doctor] Have you noticed any neurological symptoms, such as headaches?\n\n[patient] Yes, I mentioned earlier that I\'ve had some headaches.\n\n[doctor] Okay, I see. Let\'s do a quick physical exam. How are you feeling overall? Any fever, chills, or weight loss?\n\n[patient] No, I\'m feeling fine, just a bit concerned about my blood pressure.\n\n[doctor] Alright, let\'s take a look. Your neck feels supple, no swelling or lymph nodes. Your lungs sound clear, no wheezing or rales. Your heart sounds normal, and there\'s a systolic ejection murmur, but it\'s not concerning. Your abdomen is normal, and there\'s a healed scar on your left flank from the kidney transplant. No swelling in your legs. Everything looks good.\n\n[patient] Okay, good to hear.\n\n[doctor] Now, let\'s go over some test results. Your creatinine levels are normal, and your electrocardiogram looks normal as well.\n\n[patient] That\'s good to hear.\n\n[doctor] Based on your blood pressure and the results of your tests, it seems like you have hypertension. I\'m going to recommend some changes to your treatment plan. I\'d like to add carvedilol 25 mg twice a day to help bring your blood pressure down.\n\n[patient] Okay, that sounds good.\n\n[doctor] We\'ll also do some additional testing to get a lipid panel and metabolic panel to get a better picture of your overall health.\n\n[patient] Alright, that makes sense.\n\n[doctor] Everything else with your kidney function looks good. You\'re doing well after the transplant.\n\n[patient] That\'s good to hear.\n\n[doctor] Continue taking your current immunosuppression medications as prescribed. How does that sound?\n\n[patient] Yes, I\'ll continue with my current medications.\n\n[doctor] Great. Do you have any questions or concerns about the new medication or the changes we\'re making to your treatment plan?\n\n[patient] No, I think that\'s all clear. Thank you.\n\n[doctor] You\'re welcome. I\'ll see you in a few months to check on your progress. Take care and let me know if anything changes.\n\n[patient] Thanks, I will. Bye.\n\n[doctor] Bye. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Doctor: Hi there. How can I help you today?\nPatient: Hi. Um, hi.\nDoctor: Hi. Um, what\'s going on? You\'ve got a high blood pressure check today?\nPatient: Yeah.\nDoctor: And, um, what\'s been going on with your blood pressure?\nPatient: It\'s just been high lately. I\'ve been feeling stressed at work, and I\'ve been having headaches, so I thought it might be related to the stress. But I\'ve been taking Norvasc for a while now.\nDoctor: OK.\nPatient: And I\'ve been trying to keep my blood pressure under control. But I just had a blood pressure check at home, and it was like 150 over something, and I was like, "Oh, this is not good."\nDoctor: OK. And, um, how long have you been on Norvasc?\nPatient: Um, I\'ve been on it for a while. I\'m not sure how long. A few years, I think.\nDoctor: OK.\nPatient: And have you noticed any side effects from that medication?\nPatient: Um, I don\'t think so. I don\'t think so. Um, I just don\'t know.\nDoctor: OK. And, um, are you having any other symptoms, aside from the headaches, that you\'re concerned about?\nPatient: Um, no. Just the headaches. Yeah.\nDoctor: OK. And are those headaches, are they severe? Are they throbbing?\nPatient: Yeah. They\'re pretty bad. They\'re like, they\'re throbbing, and they\'re really painful.\nDoctor: OK. And have you had any chest pain or shortness of breath with these headaches?\nPatient: No.\nDoctor: OK.\nPatient: And, um, have you had any shortness of breath, any difficulty breathing at all?\nPatient: No.\nDoctor: OK. And are you on any other medications, aside from Norvasc?\nPatient: Um, yeah. I\'m on a bunch of stuff. Um, I\'m on immunosuppressants, because I had a kidney transplant.\nDoctor: OK. And how long ago was your kidney transplant?\nPatient: Um, a few years ago. I don\'t know, like, four years ago.\nDoctor: OK. And are you taking any other medications for that?\nPatient: Yeah. Um, I\'m on some immunosuppressants, and I\'m on some other stuff to help control my blood pressure.\nDoctor: OK. And, um, are you having any other symptoms, aside from the headaches, that you\'re concerned about?\nPatient: No.\nDoctor: OK. And, um, have you noticed any changes in your urine output or any changes in your stools?\nPatient: Um, no.\nDoctor: OK. And any changes in your appetite or your energy levels?\nPatient: Um, I don\'t know. I just feel like I\'m really stressed and anxious a lot, so I don\'t know if that\'s related to anything.\nDoctor: OK.\nPatient: But I just feel really anxious and stressed all the time.\nDoctor: OK. And, um, do you have any other medical problems?\nPatient: Um, I have high blood pressure.\nDoctor: OK.\nPatient: And I have a kidney transplant, and I\'m on immunosuppressants.\nDoctor: OK. And are you having any problems with your kidney function?\nPatient: Um, no. I don\'t think so. I don\'t think so.\nDoctor: OK. And have you had any recent blood tests or any other tests done recently?\nPatient: Um, I don\'t know. I think I had a blood test recently, but I don\'t know what the results were.\nDoctor: OK. And, um, are you taking any other medications for your high blood pressure?\nPatient: Yeah. I\'m on Norvasc.\nDoctor: OK. And how often are you taking that?\nPatient: Um, I take it every day.\nDoctor: OK. And what\'s the dose?\nPatient: Um, I think it\'s 10 mg.\nDoctor: OK. And, um, have you had any problems with your kidneys since your transplant?\nPatient: Um, no. I don\'t think so. I don\'t think so.\nDoctor: OK. And, um, are you having any problems with your urine output?\nPatient: Um, no. I don\'t think so. I don\'t think so.\nDoctor: OK. And any changes in your stools?\nPatient: Um, no.\nDoctor: OK. And any changes in your appetite or your energy levels?\nPatient: Um, I don\'t know. I just feel really stressed and anxious a lot, so I don\'t know if that\'s related to anything.\nDoctor: OK. And, um, do you have any other medical problems?\nPatient: Um, I have high blood pressure.\nDoctor: OK. And are you having any problems with your blood pressure at home?\nPatient: Um, yeah. It\'s just been really high lately.\nDoctor: OK. And how high is it been?\nPatient: Um, I think it\'s been over 150.\nDoctor: OK. And what\'s your usual blood pressure?\nPatient: Um, I\'m not sure. I don\'t know.\nDoctor: OK. And, um, are you having any chest pain or shortness of breath?\nPatient: No.\nDoctor: OK. And any numbness or tingling in your arms or legs?\nPatient: No.\nDoctor: OK. And any weakness in your muscles?\nPatient: No.\nDoctor: OK. And any problems with your vision?\nPatient: No.\nDoctor: OK. And any problems with your hearing?\nPatient: No.\nDoctor: OK. And any problems with your balance?\nPatient: No.\nDoctor: OK. And any problems with your bladder?\nPatient: No.\nDoctor: OK. And any problems with your bowel movements?\nPatient: No.\nDoctor: OK. And any problems with your skin?\nPatient: No.\nDoctor: OK. And any problems with your hair loss?\nPatient: No.\nDoctor: OK. And any problems with your weight?\nPatient: No.\nDoctor: OK. And any problems with your appetite?\nPatient: No.\nDoctor: OK. And any problems with your energy levels?\nPatient: No.\nDoctor: OK. And any problems with your mood?\nPatient: No.