[92mlitellm.acompletion(model='gpt-4o', api_base=None, messages=[{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a fair judge assistant assigned to deliver insightful feedback that compares individual performances, highlighting how each stands relative to others within the same cohort.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of two responses strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, choose a better response between Response A and Response B. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: "(write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (A or B)"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###Instruction:\n\nImagine you are a medical professional tasked with evaluating simulated doctor-patient conversations generated from summary notes. \nThese conversations should accurately reconstruct interactions based on the SOAP (Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan) format provided in the notes.\n\nHere is the medical note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAbnormal labs.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nThe patient is a 31-year-old female with a past medical history significant for type 2 diabetes, and hypertension who presents with abnormal labs. She had an insect bite at her last visit after hiking in the woods. We drew a Lyme titer which came back positive.\n\nThe patient reports she has been feeling tired and lacking a lot of energy. She also notes swollen elbow joints, body aches, bullseye rash, and nausea. She states she has not been able to eat much. What she does eat has been bland foods such as crackers. She denies any high fevers.\n\nRegarding her type 2 diabetes, she states her blood sugars have been okay. She notes they are sometimes a little high or low, but nothing too far out of range. She is still taking metformin.\n\nRegarding her hypertension, she is monitoring her blood pressure at home and it has been good. She is avoiding salty foods and is still taking lisinopril.\n\nThe patient denies abdominal pain and diarrhea.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Denies fevers. Endorses fatigue.\n• Gastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain and diarrhea. Endorses nausea.\n• Musculoskeletal: Endorses bilateral elbow joint swelling, pain. Endorses body aches.\n• Integumentary: Endorses a rash.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• Gastrointestinal: Examination of her abdomen reveals warmth to palpation. Nontender to palpation. No evidence of cellulitis.\n• Musculoskeletal: Swelling and pain to palpation of elbow joints bilaterally.\n• Integumentary: Small bullseye rash on the left abdominal quadrant.\n\nRESULTS\n\nLyme titer: elevated.\n\nLabs: WBC is within normal limits. All other labs are normal as well.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nThe patient is a 31-year-old female with a past medical history significant for type 2 diabetes, and hypertension who presents with abnormal labs.\n\nNewly diagnosed Lyme disease.\n• Medical Reasoning: This is a new issue for her. She presented with an insect bite at her last visit, and her subsequent lyme titer was elevated.\n• Medical Treatment: I am going to prescribe doxycycline 100 mg twice a day for 3 weeks. I am going to touch base with an infectious disease doctor to ensure nothing else that needs to be done. I want to see her again next week for follow-up for this.\n\nHypertension.\n• Medical Reasoning: This has been well-controlled based on home monitoring. She has been compliant with dietary modifications including limiting her sodium intake.\n• Additional Testing: I am going to order a lipid panel.\n• Medical Treatment: She will continue lisinopril 10 mg a day. This was refilled today.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to continue to regularly monitor her blood pressures at home.\n\nDiabetes type 2.\n• Medical Reasoning: She has noticed intermittent elevations of her blood glucose levels but has been compliant with the use of metformin. Her diet has been limited due to nausea in the setting of Lyme disease.\n• Additional Testing: I am going to order a hemoglobin A1c to ensure no medication changes are needed.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor]: Hi there, how are you doing today?\n\n[patient]: Hi, I\'m not doing great. I\'ve been feeling really tired and lacking a lot of energy.\n\n[doctor]: Oh, sorry to hear that. Can you tell me more about what\'s been going on?\n\n[patient]: Sure. I had an insect bite after hiking in the woods last week, and my doctor drew some blood to test for Lyme disease.\n\n[doctor]: Ah, that\'s interesting. Did you know the results?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I did. The test came back positive.\n\n[doctor]: Okay, so you\'ve been bitten by an infected tick. How have you been feeling since then?\n\n[patient]: I\'ve been feeling really tired and lacking a lot of energy. I also have swollen elbow joints, body aches, and a bullseye rash.\n\n[doctor]: Hmm, that sounds pretty uncomfortable. Have you had any high fevers?\n\n[patient]: No, I haven\'t had any high fevers.\n\n[doctor]: Alright. How about your blood sugars? How have they been?\n\n[patient]: They\'ve been okay, but sometimes a little high or low, but nothing too far out of range. I\'m still taking metformin.\n\n[doctor]: Good to hear that your diabetes is under control. How about your hypertension? How\'s your blood pressure been?\n\n[patient]: My blood pressure has been good. I\'ve been monitoring it at home and it\'s been within range. I\'ve been avoiding salty foods and still taking lisinopril.\n\n[doctor]: Great job with your blood pressure control. Have you had any abdominal pain or diarrhea?\n\n[patient]: No, I haven\'t had any abdominal pain or diarrhea.\n\n[doctor]: Okay. Let\'s go through a review of your systems. How have you been feeling overall?\n\n[patient]: I feel tired all the time.\n\n[doctor]: Any nausea?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I\'ve been feeling nauseous.\n\n[doctor]: Any pain or discomfort in your abdomen?\n\n[patient]: No, no abdominal pain.\n\n[doctor]: How about your joints? Any pain or swelling?\n\n[patient]: Yes, my elbows are swollen and painful.\n\n[doctor]: Any other joint pain?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I feel body aches all over.\n\n[doctor]: And what about any rashes or skin issues?\n\n[patient]: I have a bullseye rash on my left abdominal quadrant.\n\n[doctor]: Alright, let\'s take a look. (Examines the rash) It looks like a bullseye rash, consistent with Lyme disease. Let\'s do a physical exam to check your abdomen. (Examines the abdomen) Your abdomen feels warm to the touch, but it\'s not tender. No signs of cellulitis.\n\n[patient]: Okay.\n\n[doctor]: I\'ll also check your joints. Your elbow joints are swollen and painful to the touch. Let\'s take a look at your rash again. (Examines the rash) It\'s a small bullseye rash on your left abdominal quadrant.\n\n[patient]: Yes, that\'s the one.\n\n[doctor]: Alright, your labs showed an elevated Lyme titer. Your white blood cell count is within normal limits, and all other labs are normal. We\'ll start you on doxycycline 100 mg twice a day for 3 weeks. I\'ll also touch base with an infectious disease doctor to ensure we don\'t need to do anything else.\n\n[patient]: Okay, what else?\n\n[doctor]: For your hypertension, you\'ve been doing well with your blood pressure control, and you\'ve been compliant with dietary modifications, including limiting your sodium intake. I\'ll order a lipid panel to check your cholesterol levels.\n\n[patient]: Alright.\n\n[doctor]: You\'ll continue taking lisinopril 10 mg a day. I\'ve refilled your prescription today. It\'s important to keep monitoring your blood pressures at home.\n\n[patient]: I\'ll keep doing that.\n\n[doctor]: For your type 2 diabetes, you\'ve noticed intermittent elevations of your blood glucose levels, but you\'ve been compliant with the use of metformin. Your diet has been limited due to nausea in the setting of Lyme disease. I\'ll order a hemoglobin A1c to ensure we don\'t need to make any medication changes.\n\n[patient]: Okay, got it.\n\n[doctor]: Do you have any questions about the treatment plan?\n\n[patient]: No, I think I understand. Doxycycline for three weeks and I\'ll keep taking my other medications.\n\n[doctor]: That\'s correct. We\'ll see you back in a week to follow up on your progress. If you have any issues or concerns, don\'t hesitate to call.\n\n[patient]: Alright, thank you.\n\n[doctor]: You\'re welcome. Take care and feel better soon.\n\n[patient]: Thank you, doctor.\n\n[doctor]: See you next week.\n\n[patient]: See you then. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Doctor: Hello?\n\nPatient: Hello.\n\nDoctor: Hi. This is Doctor. Can you please confirm your name for me?\n\nPatient: Yeah, my name is Sarah.\n\nDoctor: And your date of birth, please?\n\nPatient: 12/25/1989.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And what\'s your address, please?\n\nPatient: Uh, 123 Main Street.\n\nDoctor: OK. And how can I reach you, please?\n\nPatient: Uh, I can be reached at this number. Uh, my email is not working right now. But I can be reached at this number.\n\nDoctor: OK. And can you please confirm your medical history for me, please?\n\nPatient: Yeah, I have type 2 diabetes and hypertension.\n\nDoctor: OK. And have you had any recent illnesses recently?\n\nPatient: Yeah, I have been feeling really tired and really nauseous. And I have been having some joint pain in my elbows and my body. And I have a rash on my stomach.\n\nDoctor: OK. And have you been feeling any fevers?\n\nPatient: No, I haven\'t been having any fevers. I have just been feeling really tired and really nauseous.\n\nDoctor: OK. And ha