-d '{'model': 'gpt-4o', 'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a fair judge assistant assigned to deliver insightful feedback that compares individual performances, highlighting how each stands relative to others within the same cohort.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of two responses strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, choose a better response between Response A and Response B. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: "(write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (A or B)"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###Instruction:\n\nImagine you are a medical professional tasked with evaluating simulated doctor-patient conversations generated from summary notes. \nThese conversations should accurately reconstruct interactions based on the SOAP (Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan) format provided in the notes.\n\nHere is the medical note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nEvaluation of high blood sugar.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMs. Hannah Phillips is a 52-year-old female who presents for evaluation of high blood sugar. The patient admits to dietary indiscretion. She notes that she has been eating more sweets during the holidays. She reports that this past week, she felt really lightheaded, was seeing spots in her vision, and was really dizzy. She went to the emergency room and was told that she had high blood sugar. Ms. Phillips was given IV fluids. The patient was also instructed to get into a routine and watch her diet. She states that she is staying with family during the holidays and having trouble returning to her routine diet, but wants to make sure that her high blood sugar is not an issue.\n\nThe patient denies any fever or chills. She denies chest pain or shortness of breath. She denies abdominal pain or burning when she urinates. She denies joint pain or muscle aches.\n\nShe notes that she has osteoarthritis, and that nothing seems out of the ordinary for her joint pain.\n\nThe patient has a history of atrial fibrillation. She states that she has felt her heart racing when she is anxious; however, other than that, she has not experienced any issues. She is currently taking metoprolol 25 mg 2 times per day.\n\nRegarding her acid reflux, she states that she has been doing well. She has been taking the medication that was prescribed to her, and it has been very helpful. She is on the Protonix 40 mg once daily, and she is doing well with that\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Negative for fever, chills\n• Cardiovascular: Negative for chest pain\n• Respiratory: Negative for shortness of breath.\n• Gastrointestinal: Negative for abdominal pain.\n• Genitourinary: No dysuria.\n• Musculoskeletal: No Arthralgias, Myalgias.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\nConstitutional\n• General Appearance: appear in no distress.\n\nNeck\n• General Examination: Her thyroid is a little enlarged but nontender. There are no carotid bruits. Respiratory\n• Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally. Cardiovascular\n• Auscultation of Heart: Irregular rate and rhythm. Musculoskeletal\n• Examination of Abdomen: Nice soft and have no edema in lower extremities.\n\nRESULTS\n\nGlucose: elevated.\nHbA1c: elevated.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nMs. Hannah Phillips is a 52-year-old female who presents for evaluation of high blood sugar.\n\nDiabetes.\n• Medical Reasoning: Elevated blood glucose and hemoglobin A1c levels based on recent labs.\n• Additional Testing: We will order a complete metabolic panel for further evaluation.\n• Medical Treatment: Initiate metformin 500 mg twice daily. Encouraged dietary modifications. The patient will be given a glucometer to monitor her glucose levels at home.\n\nAtrial Fibrillation.\n\n• Medical Reasoning: Asymptomatic and well-controlled at this time.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue metoprolol 25 mg twice daily. Refilled today.\n\nHistory of Reflux.\n\n• Medical Reasoning: Well-controlled on current regimen.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue Protonix 40 mg daily.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor]: Hi there, how are you doing today?\n\n[patient]: Hi, I\'m okay. I\'ve been feeling a bit off lately.\n\n[doctor]: Oh, sorry to hear that. What\'s been going on that\'s making you feel off?\n\n[patient]: Well, I\'ve been eating a lot of sweets during the holidays, and I\'ve been feeling really lightheaded, seeing spots in my vision, and feeling really dizzy.\n\n[doctor]: Hmm, that sounds like you\'re having some symptoms of high blood sugar. Have you noticed any fever or chills?\n\n[patient]: No, I haven\'t had a fever or chills.\n\n[doctor]: Okay, and have you had any chest pain or shortness of breath?\n\n[patient]: No, I haven\'t had any chest pain or shortness of breath.\n\n[doctor]: Alright, have you had any abdominal pain or burning when you urinate?\n\n[patient]: No, I haven\'t had any abdominal pain or burning when I urinate.\n\n[doctor]: How about joint pain or muscle aches?\n\n[patient]: I have osteoarthritis, and it\'s been acting up a bit, but nothing seems out of the ordinary for my joint pain.\n\n[doctor]: Got it. You have a history of atrial fibrillation. How have you been feeling about that?\n\n[patient]: I feel like my heart has been racing when I\'m anxious, but other than that, I haven\'t had any issues.\n\n[doctor]: Alright, and are you currently taking any medication for your atrial fibrillation?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I\'m taking metoprolol 25 mg twice a day.\n\n[doctor]: Okay, that\'s good. How about your acid reflux? How\'s that been?\n\n[patient]: I\'ve been doing well with it. I\'m taking the medication that was prescribed, Protonix 40 mg once a day, and it\'s been very helpful.\n\n[doctor]: That\'s great to hear. Let\'s go through a review of your systems now. Have you had any fever or chills?\n\n[patient]: No, I haven\'t had a fever or chills.\n\n[doctor]: Okay, any chest pain?\n\n[patient]: No, no chest pain.\n\n[doctor]: Any shortness of breath?\n\n[patient]: No, I haven\'t had any shortness of breath.\n\n[doctor]: Any abdominal pain?\n\n[patient]: No, I haven\'t had any abdominal pain.\n\n[doctor]: Any dysuria?\n\n[patient]: No, no dysuria.\n\n[doctor]: Any joint pain or muscle aches?\n\n[patient]: No, no joint pain or muscle aches.\n\n[doctor]: Let\'s do a physical examination. Your general appearance is good, and you appear to be in no distress. Your thyroid is a bit enlarged but not tender. There are no carotid bruits. Your lungs are clear bilaterally. Your heart has an irregular rate and rhythm, but that\'s consistent with your atrial fibrillation. Your abdomen is nice and soft, and there\'s no edema in your lower extremities.\n\n[patient]: Okay, that\'s all good to know, I guess.\n\n[doctor]: Now, let\'s talk about your lab results. Your glucose levels are elevated, and your hemoglobin A1c is also elevated.\n\n[patient]: Oh, that\'s not good.\n\n[doctor]: Yes, it is concerning, but we can work on getting those under control. We\'ll order a complete metabolic panel for further evaluation. Based on your results, I\'m going to initiate metformin 500 mg twice daily to help manage your blood sugar levels. It\'s important to make some dietary modifications as well.\n\n[patient]: Okay, I\'ll do my best.\n\n[doctor]: Also, I\'ll give you a glucometer to monitor your glucose levels at home. This will help us track your progress and make adjustments to your treatment plan as needed.\n\n[patient]: Alright, that sounds like a good idea.\n\n[doctor]: In terms of your atrial fibrillation, it seems to be well-controlled at this time. You\'ll continue taking metoprolol 25 mg twice daily. I\'ll refill that for you today.\n\n[patient]: Okay, great.\n\n[doctor]: Your acid reflux has been well-controlled on your current regimen, so we\'ll continue with Protonix 40 mg daily.\n\n[patient]: That\'s good to know.\n\n[doctor]: Do you have any questions about the treatment plan?\n\n[patient]: No, I think I understand. I\'ll make sure to follow the diet and take the medication as prescribed.\n\n[doctor]: Excellent. I\'ll see you in six weeks for a follow-up. If you have any concerns or notice any worsening symptoms, don\'t hesitate to reach out. Do you have any questions about the glucometer or how to use it?\n\n[patient]: No, I think I can figure it out.\n\n[doctor]: Great. We\'ll see you in six weeks.\n\n[patient]: Thanks, I\'ll see you then.\n\n[doctor]: Take care, and have a happy holiday season. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Doctor: Hi. How are you doing today?\n\nPatient: Hi. I\'m doing fine. Thank you.\n\nDoctor: Good. Hi. Uh, hi. Hi, I\'m Dr. Smith. I\'m a doctor from your primary care office. Um, hi. Um, how are you doing today?\n\nPatient: Hi. I\'m doing fine. Thank you.\n\nDoctor: Good. Um, hi. So, um, hi. So, um, how are you doing today? Um, what brings you in today?\n\nPatient: Um, I\'m having some, um, some high blood sugar.\n\nDoctor: Okay. Um, okay. So, how high is your blood sugar?\n\nPatient: Um, I\'m not sure. I don\'t know. I was in the emergency room a week ago, and they told me that my blood sugar was like, really high, but they didn\'t give me any numbers. And then they told me to go to my doctor and get some medicine, and I didn\'t have any medicine.\n\nDoctor: Okay. Okay. So, you went to the emergency room, and they told you your blood sugar was high, and they didn\'t give you any numbers, but they told you to follow up with your doctor. And then what happened?\n\nPatient: And then I went to my doctor, and she told me to, um, to take some medi