[92mlitellm.acompletion(model='gpt-4o', api_base=None, messages=[{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a fair judge assistant assigned to deliver insightful feedback that compares individual performances, highlighting how each stands relative to others within the same cohort.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of two responses strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, choose a better response between Response A and Response B. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: "(write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (A or B)"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###Instruction:\n\nImagine you are a medical professional tasked with evaluating simulated doctor-patient conversations generated from summary notes. \nThese conversations should accurately reconstruct interactions based on the SOAP (Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan) format provided in the notes.\n\nHere is the medical note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow up irritable bowel syndrome (IBS).\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMs. Brittany Edwards is a pleasant 32-year-old female who returns in follow up for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) with functional abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting.\n\nMs. Edwards was last seen in August 2019 with flares of abdominal pain and was diagnosed with irritable bowel syndrome that was treated with Bentyl. She had a previous prescription for Reglan that she received from the emergency room that she used as needed for nausea. She was instructed to start a low dose fiber supplement such as Citrucel daily, probiotics to help with gas and bloating, and Bentyl up to 4 times daily for intestinal cramping. She was instructed to discontinue Reglan and to use Zofran as needed for nausea. Neuromodulators such as Elavil and Buspar were discussed but not started.\n\nMs. Edwards continues to have intermittent abdominal symptoms. Monday through Thursday she has less flare ups of her symptoms but states she is currently having cramps and sharp pains. She describes she recently ate shrimp which caused her to have both upper and lower abdominal pain and bloating. However, she notices episodes of diarrhea after eating chicken alfredo and consuming alcohol. She does not believe the alfredo contributes to her symptoms. The patient states she consumes 2 drinks when out of either tequila or brown liquor and occasionally a martini. She does her best to avoid “heavy foods,” but ate fried chicken today without any symptoms. She has stopped drinking sodas which has been helpful.\u202fThe patient reports passing 2-3 formed stools a day.\n\nREVIEW OF SYMPTOMS\n\n•\u202fGastrointestinal:\u202fPositive nausea, diarrhea, and abdominal pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nAlert and cooperative, appears stated age.\n\nRespiratory\nLungs clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\nHeart regular rate and rhythm.\n\nGastrointestinal\nAbdomen soft, non-tender, no masses or organomegaly. Normoactive bowel sounds.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Irritable bowel syndrome\n\nBrittany Edwards is a 32-year-old established patient who returned today to discuss management of IBS and functional abdominal pain made worse by high stress, poor eating habits, and poor sleeping habits.\n\nPLAN\n\nI have encouraged dietary and lifestyle modifications as well as starting a neuromodulator, such as Elavil 25 mg at bedtime for IBS. She can use\u202fBentyl\u202f20 mg as needed 30-45 minutes\u202fbefore eating out at a restaurant. She can also use Zofran as needed for nausea. However, I have encouraged her to reduce greasy fatty foods and alcohol consumption.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nSend MyChart message in 1 month regarding any side effects of issues with Elavil or if dose adjustments are needed.\nReturn to clinic in 1 month or sooner if needed.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor] Good morning, how are you doing today?\n\n[patient] Hi, I\'m doing okay, but I\'m still having some issues with my irritable bowel syndrome.\n\n[doctor] I see. Let\'s discuss your symptoms. How have you been feeling lately?\n\n[patient] I\'ve been having a lot of abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting.\n\n[doctor] Okay, I understand. You were last seen in August 2019, and we discussed your irritable bowel syndrome and functional abdominal pain. We started you on Bentyl, a low dose fiber supplement, probiotics, and instructed you to discontinue Reglan and use Zofran as needed for nausea. We also discussed starting neuromodulators such as Elavil and Buspar, but we decided not to start them yet.\n\n[patient] Yes, that\'s right. I\'ve been taking the Bentyl as prescribed.\n\n[doctor] Good. How have you been managing your symptoms?\n\n[patient] I\'ve been having some flare-ups, but they\'re not as bad as they were before. I\'ve been taking the Bentyl and the probiotics, and it seems to help a bit.\n\n[doctor] That\'s good to hear. Can you tell me more about your symptoms? Have you noticed any specific triggers or patterns?\n\n[patient] Well, I\'ve been having less flare-ups from Monday to Thursday, but this week I\'m having some cramps and sharp pains. I ate some shrimp yesterday and it caused upper and lower abdominal pain and bloating.\n\n[doctor] Hmm, interesting. Have you noticed any other food triggers or patterns?\n\n[patient] Yes, I get diarrhea after eating chicken alfredo, but I don\'t think that\'s related to my symptoms. I also drink 2 drinks when I\'m out, either tequila or brown liquor, and occasionally a martini. I try to avoid heavy foods, but I had fried chicken today and didn\'t have any symptoms.\n\n[doctor] Alright. It sounds like you\'ve been trying to make some dietary changes. How about your soda intake? Have you cut down?\n\n[patient] Yes, I\'ve stopped drinking soda and that seems to have helped.\n\n[doctor] That\'s good. How often do you go to the bathroom?\n\n[patient] I usually go 2-3 times a day and it\'s all formed stools.\n\n[doctor] Okay. I\'ll review your symptoms again. You\'re experiencing gastrointestinal symptoms like nausea, diarrhea, and abdominal pain. \n\n[patient] Yes, that\'s right.\n\n[doctor] Let\'s do a quick physical exam. Your vital signs are all normal, your lungs are clear, and your heart rate and rhythm are regular. Your abdomen is soft and non-tender, and there are no masses or organomegaly. Your bowel sounds are normoactive.\n\n[patient] Okay.\n\n[doctor] Based on your symptoms and exam, I think you continue to have irritable bowel syndrome. We\'ll need to continue managing your symptoms with some lifestyle and dietary changes.\n\n[patient] What do you recommend?\n\n[doctor] I\'m going to start you on a neuromodulator, Elavil 25 mg at bedtime, which may help with your IBS symptoms. You can also use Bentyl 20 mg as needed 30-45 minutes before eating out at a restaurant. If you need to use Zofran for nausea, you can do so as well.\n\n[patient] Okay, I\'ll try that.\n\n[doctor] I also want you to avoid greasy, fatty foods and cut back on alcohol consumption.\n\n[patient] Alright, I\'ll try to reduce those.\n\n[doctor] Great. We\'ll need to monitor your response to the Elavil and see if any adjustments are needed. I\'ll send you a MyChart message in a month to check on your progress. If you need to come back sooner or have any side effects, please don\'t hesitate to reach out.\n\n[patient] I\'ll do that. Thank you.\n\n[doctor] You\'re welcome. See you in a month, or sooner if needed. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Doctor: Hi, welcome to the clinic. My name is Dr. Smith and I\'m a GP. Can you confirm your name and date of birth for me, please?\nPatient: Hi. Um, my name is Brittany Edwards, and my date of birth is September 14th, 1989.\nDoctor: OK, thank you. Can you confirm your address for me, please?\nPatient: I live at 1234 Main Street, Anytown, USA.\nDoctor: OK, thank you. Um, can you tell me a bit about your symptoms, please?\nPatient: OK. So, um, I\'ve been having a lot of stomach pain and nausea and vomiting. I\'ve been having these episodes, um, about every other week. Um, and I\'ve been taking Bentyl for the past few months, and I\'ve been taking it four times a day. But I still have these episodes.\nDoctor: OK. And can you tell me more about the pain you\'ve been having? Is it just in your tummy, or is it anywhere else?\nPatient: Um, it\'s just in my tummy. It\'s usually in the lower part of my stomach. It\'s like a sharp pain. It\'s not really a dull ache. It\'s like a sharp pain. And it\'s usually accompanied by nausea and vomiting. And sometimes I get diarrhea.\nDoctor: OK. And how often do you have diarrhea?\nPatient: Um, I would say, um, usually two to three times a day. It\'s not always diarrhea, it\'s just like loose stools.\nDoctor: OK. And do you have any, uh, any blood in your stool at all?\nPatient: No, I don\'t have any blood in my stool.\nDoctor: OK. And do you have any, uh, any mucus in your stool at all?\nPatient: No, I don\'t have any mucus in my stool.\nDoctor: OK. And do you have any, uh, any abdominal cramps?\nPatient: Yes, I do have abdominal cramps. It\'s like, um, it\'s like my stomach is just cramping up and it\'s really uncomfortable.\nDoctor: OK. And how long have you had these symptoms for?\nPatient: Um, I\'ve had them for about three months now.\nDoctor: OK. And have you noticed any triggers for your symptoms?\nPatient: Um, I think it\'s usually when I eat greasy foods, like fried foods or foods that are high in fat. And also, um, when I drink too much, I think that triggers it too.\nDoctor: OK. And do you drink a lot of soda?\nPatient: Um, I used to drink a lot of soda, but I\'ve been trying to cut back on it. I\'ve been drinking more water now.\nDoctor: OK. And do you have any, uh, any stress in your life that you\'re not managing well?\nPatient: Yeah, I have a lot of stress. I have a lot of anxiety. I work as a nurse and I have a lot of stress at work. And also, um, I have a lot of stress at home. My husband and I have a lot of disagreements.\nDoctor: OK. And how do you usually manage your stress?\nPatient: Um, I try to take deep breaths and I try to meditate. I also try to exercise. I like to go for walks.\nDoctor: OK. And do you have any other symptoms that you\'ve noticed? Any other symptoms that you\'ve noticed in your body, like, uh, any joint pain, any muscle pain?\nPatient: No, I don\'t have any joint pain or muscle pain.\nDoctor: OK. And do you have any, uh, any headaches?\nPatient: Um, I do have headaches. But I don\'t know if it\'s related to my stomach pain or not.\nDoctor: OK. And do you have any, uh, any fever?\nPatient: No, I don\'t have a fever.\nDoctor: OK. And do you have any, uh, any chills?\nPatient: No, I don\'t have chills.\nDoctor: OK. And do you have any, uh, any vomiting?\nPatient: Yes, I do have vomiting. But I don\'t know if it\'s related to my stomach pain or not.\nDoctor: OK. And do you have any, uh, any diarrhea?\nPatient: Yes, I do have diarrhea.\nDoctor: OK. And do you have any, uh, any abdominal pain?\nPatient: Yes, I do have abdominal pain.\nDoctor: OK. And do you have any, uh, any constipation?\nPatient: No, I don\'t have constipation.\nDoctor: OK. And do you have any, uh, any difficulty with your bowels?\nPatient: Um, I do have difficulty with my bowels. I have trouble going to the bathroom. It\'s like my bowels are constipated.\nDoctor: OK. And have you had any, uh, any weight loss?\nPatient: No, I haven\'t had any weight loss.\nDoctor: OK. And have you had any, uh, any night sweats?\nPatient: No, I haven\'t had any night sweats.\nDoctor: OK. And have you had any, uh, any hot flashes?\nPatient: No, I haven\'t had any hot flashes.\nDoctor: OK. And have you had any, uh, any vaginal bleeding?\nPatient: No, I haven\'t had any vaginal bleeding.\nDoctor: OK. And have you had any, uh, any vaginal discharge?\nPatient: No, I haven\'t had any vaginal discharge.\nDoctor: OK. And have you had any, uh, any abdominal masses?\nPatient: No, I don\'t have any abdominal masses.\nDoctor: OK. And have you had any, uh, any abdominal tenderness?\nPatient: Yes, I do have abdominal tenderness.\nDoctor: OK. And have you had any, uh, any abdominal distension?\nPatient: Yes, I do have abdominal distension.\nDoctor: OK. And have you had any, uh, any abdominal guarding?\nPatient: Yes, I do have abdominal guarding.\nDoctor: OK. And have you had any, uh, any abdominal rebound tenderness?\nPatient: Yes, I do have abdominal rebound tenderness.\nDoctor: OK. And have you had any, uh, any abdominal masses that you can feel?\nPatient: No, I don\'t have any abdominal masses that I can feel.\nDoctor: OK. And have you had any, uh, any abdominal tenderness that you can feel?\nPatient: Yes, I do have abdominal tenderness that I can feel.\nDoctor: OK. And have you had any, uh, any abdominal distension that you can feel?\nPatient: Yes, I do have abdominal distension that I can feel.\nDoctor: OK. And have you had any, uh, any abdominal guarding that you can feel?\nPatient: Yes, I do have abdominal guarding that I can feel.\nDoctor: OK. And have you had any, uh, any abdominal rebound tenderness that you can feel?\nPatient: Yes, I do have abdominal rebound tenderness that I can feel.\nDoctor: OK. And have you had any, uh, any abdominal masses that you can feel?\nPatient: No, I don\'t have any abdominal masses that I can feel.\nDoctor: OK. And have you had any, uh, any abdominal tenderness that you can feel?\nPatient: Yes, I do have abdominal tenderness that I can feel.\nDoctor: OK. And have you had any, uh, any abdominal distension that you can feel?\nPatient: Yes, I do have abdominal distension that I can feel.\nDoctor: OK. And have you had any, uh, 