-d '{'model': 'gpt-4o', 'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a fair judge assistant assigned to deliver insightful feedback that compares individual performances, highlighting how each stands relative to others within the same cohort.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of two responses strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, choose a better response between Response A and Response B. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: "(write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (A or B)"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###Instruction:\n\nImagine you are a medical professional tasked with evaluating simulated doctor-patient conversations generated from summary notes. \nThese conversations should accurately reconstruct interactions based on the SOAP (Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan) format provided in the notes.\n\nHere is the medical note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nHypertension.\nAlcohol use disorder.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nAmanda Taylor is an 72-year-old woman who presents for a follow-up for hypertension. History includes significant alcohol use disorder and mild hypercholesterolemia. At last visit on April 16, BP was 130/90. After presenting at 150/100 she was prescribed hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg by mouth once daily and lisinopril 20 mg by mouth once daily. CMP and CBC were essentially unremarkable.\n\nMrs. Taylor admits to consuming alcohol 4-5 times a week and is interested in Naltrexone injections. She acknowledges that her blood pressure would likely improve if she stopped alcohol consumption. She is checking her blood pressure in the morning and evening, stating it is higher at night but will improve after sitting for a few minutes. She is averaged 135-145 or 150 over 95-110.\n\nShe experienced side effects from lisinopril, such as headaches, “swooshing” in her left ear, and lethargy. She took half a dose and did not experience the side effects.\n\nPAST HISTORY\n\nMedical\nHypertension.\nHypercholesterolemia.\nAlcohol use disorder.\n\nCURRENT MEDICATIONS\n\nLisinopril 20 mg tablet once daily.\nHydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg once daily.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nConstitutional\nNo distress. No fever. No tobacco or other substance use.\n\nNeurological\nAlert.\n\nPsychological\nShe does not appear anxious, agitated or depressed.\n\nNeck\nJVP flat with a head at 90 degrees.\n\nRespiratory\nNo cough or shortness of breath. Chest clear to percussion and auscultation. Unlabored breathing.\n\nCardiovascular\nNo CVA tenderness. Cardiac rhythm regular. No murmur. No gallop.\n\nMusculoskeletal\nNo ankle edema.\n\nIntegumentary\nSkin is moist. Good trigger.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Alcohol use disorder.\n• Hypertension.\n\nMrs. Taylor presents for a follow-up for hypertension. History includes significant alcohol use disorder and mild hypercholesterolemia. At last visit on April 16, BP was 130/90. After presenting at 150/100 she was prescribed hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg by mouth once daily and lisinopril 20 mg by mouth once daily. CMP and CBC were essentially unremarkable.\n\nPLAN\n\nAlcohol use disorder\nI will start her on naltrexone which we have previously discussed. She will take the injectable form and start on May 17. We reviewed side effects and risks. The potential benefit of alcohol cessation would be highly beneficial to the patient.\n\nHypertension\nShe will continue taking lisinopril 10 mg/day and hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg daily. Quite possibly her blood pressure will improve significantly if she is able to completely stop consuming alcohol. Target BP is 130-140/70-80. I will see her back week of June 14 which likely will be the time of her second naltrexone injection.\n\nRecommend upgrading influenza vaccination and Shingrix #2, she defers this.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nCall the clinic with any questions. Make follow up appointment week of June 14th.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor] Hello, how are you doing today?\n\n[patient] Hi, I\'m doing okay. Just a bit concerned about my blood pressure.\n\n[doctor] Ah, I understand. How high has it been?\n\n[patient] It\'s been around 135-145 over 95-110.\n\n[doctor] Hmm, that\'s a bit higher than we\'d like. Do you check it regularly?\n\n[patient] Yes, I do. I check it in the morning and evening.\n\n[doctor] That\'s good. Do you notice any patterns? Is it higher at any particular time of day?\n\n[patient] Yes, it\'s usually higher at night.\n\n[doctor] Okay. And does it improve if you sit for a few minutes?\n\n[patient] Yes, it does. It gets better after I sit for a bit.\n\n[doctor] Alright. So we\'ll need to work on reducing your blood pressure. I\'m going to prescribe some additional medication to help with that.\n\n[patient] Okay.\n\n[doctor] I\'m going to start you on naltrexone injections for your alcohol use disorder. Have you been drinking much lately?\n\n[patient] Yeah, I drink about 4-5 times a week.\n\n[doctor] I see. And are you interested in stopping or reducing your drinking?\n\n[patient] Yes, I am. I know my blood pressure would improve if I stopped.\n\n[doctor] That\'s great to hear. Naltrexone can help with that. We\'ll start the injections on May 17. Are you willing to give it a try?\n\n[patient] Yes, I am.\n\n[doctor] Good. We\'ll also continue the lisinopril 20 mg and hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg daily. It\'s important to take these medications regularly.\n\n[patient] Okay, I\'ll keep taking them.\n\n[doctor] How have you been feeling on the lisinopril?\n\n[patient] Honestly, I\'ve been experiencing some side effects like headaches, this weird sensation in my left ear, and feeling lethargic.\n\n[doctor] Hmm, those are common side effects. Have you noticed any improvement when you took half the dose?\n\n[patient] Yes, the side effects went away.\n\n[doctor] That\'s good to know. We\'ll keep monitoring your blood pressure closely.\n\n[patient] Okay.\n\n[doctor] Do you have any other medical conditions?\n\n[patient] No, just hypertension, hypercholesterolemia, and my alcohol use disorder.\n\n[doctor] Alright. Any other symptoms or concerns?\n\n[patient] No, nothing else.\n\n[doctor] Good. Your physical exam today shows you appear to be in no distress, no fever, no tobacco or other substance use. Your neurological exam is normal, and you don\'t appear anxious, agitated, or depressed. Your neck veins are flat with your head at 90 degrees, your respiratory exam is clear, and your heart sounds are normal with no murmurs or gallop. You have no ankle edema and your skin is moist and good.\n\n[patient] Okay.\n\n[doctor] Based on your history and physical exam, I’m going to diagnose you with alcohol use disorder and hypertension. We’ll start the naltrexone injections and continue the lisinopril 10 mg and hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg daily. If you can completely stop consuming alcohol, your blood pressure should improve significantly.\n\n[patient] That sounds good.\n\n[doctor] We’ll also upgrade your influenza vaccination and Shingrix #2. Do you have any questions?\n\n[patient] No, I think I understand.\n\n[doctor] Great. Make sure to call the clinic if you have any questions or concerns. Your next follow-up is scheduled for the week of June 14th, which will be the time of your second naltrexone injection. Do you have any other questions or concerns?\n\n[patient] No, I think that\'s it.\n\n[doctor] Alright. See you on June 14th.\n\n[patient] Thanks, doctor.\n\n[doctor] Take care, and see you soon. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Patient: Hi.\nDoctor: Hello?\nPatient: Hi.\nDoctor: Hi. Hi.\nPatient: Hi.\nDoctor: Hi. Hi, hello.\nPatient: Hi.\nDoctor: Hi. Hi, hel