rally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm. No ectopic beats. No murmurs, clicks, or rubs.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination of Abdomen: Soft, nondistended abdomen. Positive slight guarding to the right upper quadrant, but without rebound tenderness. Positive for Murphy signs. Peritoneal signs not appreciated.\n- Auscultation: Bowel sounds normal in all 4 quadrants.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAbdominal ultrasound obtained at an outside facility is reviewed today. This demonstrates multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder. I do appreciate some mild thickening. The common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Mild cholecystitis with gallstones.\n- Medical Reasoning: Based on the patient\'s symptoms, exam findings, and ultrasound, I do believe he has some mild cholecystitis with gallstones. I do not appreciate any frank obstruction, but it looks like his gallbladder is inflamed. This appears to have happened maybe over the last few weeks or since he has been symptomatic.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I had a thorough discussion with the patient concerning surgical treatment. The preoperative, intraoperative, and postoperative courses of care were described and discussed. I explained the surgery will take approximately 1 to 1.5 hours, and he will probably spend the night in the hospital for post-procedure monitoring. We also discussed that he should avoid high fat foods after surgery and eat foods that are high in fiber. All questions were answered.\n- Medical Treatment: Since he has not had any improvement with dietary modifications, I recommend a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. A mutually agreed upon surgical date has been scheduled for 1 week.\n\n2. Type 2 diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is currently managing his diabetes with diet and exercise.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient he should refrain from hiking in the immediate postoperative period; however, he may return to walking outdoors on solid ground 1 week status post-surgery.\nMedical Treatment: I recommended he follow up with his physician regarding his impending surgery. We will continue to monitor his diabetes as has been outlined by either his endocrinologist or his primary care provider.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me 1 week status post-surgery.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor] Hi there, how are you doing today?\n\n[patient] Hi, I\'m okay, but I\'ve been having some abdominal pain lately.\n\n[doctor] I see. Abdominal pain, how would you describe it?\n\n[patient] It\'s on the right side, it\'s been pretty constant and it makes me feel nauseous.\n\n[doctor] Hmm, that sounds uncomfortable. Have you noticed any other symptoms like vomiting?\n\n[patient] No, I haven\'t been vomiting, just the pain and nausea.\n\n[doctor] Alright, do you have any medical conditions or chronic illnesses?\n\n[patient] I have type 2 diabetes.\n\n[doctor] Okay, thank you for letting me know. Do you have any hobbies or activities that you enjoy?\n\n[patient] I really enjoy nature photography.\n\n[doctor] That sounds wonderful. Have you had any family history of any medical conditions?\n\n[patient] Yes, both of my parents have had cholecystectomies due to gallstones.\n\n[doctor] I see. Any other medical issues or hospitalizations?\n\n[patient] No, nothing else notable.\n\n[doctor] Alright, let\'s go over your vital signs. Your blood pressure is 128/88 mmHg, respiratory rate is 16 breaths per minute, heart rate is 68 beats per minute, and oxygen saturation is 99% on room air. Your body temperature is afebrile. Any other concerns or symptoms?\n\n[patient] No, that\'s all.\n\n[doctor] Okay. Let\'s do a physical exam. I\'ll start with your respiratory system. Your lungs sound clear and equal bilaterally.\n\n[patient] Okay.\n\n[doctor] Next, I\'ll check your heart. Your heart sounds regular and has a normal rate and rhythm. No murmurs, clicks, or rubs. Now, let\'s examine your abdomen. Your abdomen is soft and not distended. I do feel some slight guarding to the right upper quadrant, but there\'s no rebound tenderness. I also did the Murphy sign, which is positive. However, I didn\'t feel any peritoneal signs.\n\n[patient] Okay.\n\n[doctor] I\'ll also listen to your bowel sounds. They\'re normal in all four quadrants. Do you feel any pain or discomfort during this exam?\n\n[patient] No, it doesn\'t hurt too much.\n\n[doctor] Alright. Let\'s review the results of an abdominal ultrasound you had at an outside facility. It shows you have multiple gallstones in your gallbladder, and there is some mild thickening. The common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.\n\n[patient] Oh, I see.\n\n[doctor] Based on your symptoms, exam findings, and ultrasound, I believe you have mild cholecystitis with gallstones. It looks like your gallbladder is inflamed, but there\'s no frank obstruction. This seems to have happened maybe over the last few weeks or since you\'ve been symptomatic.\n\n[patient] Okay, what does this mean?\n\n[doctor] I\'ll explain. You have an inflamed gallbladder due to the presence of gallstones. This is causing your abdominal pain and nausea. I recommend a laparoscopic cholecystectomy, which is a surgical procedure to remove your gallbladder.\n\n[patient] What does that entail?\n\n[doctor] The surgery will take about 1 to 1.5 hours. You\'ll likely spend the night in the hospital for post-procedure monitoring. We\'ll also advise you to avoid high-fat foods after surgery and eat foods that are high in fiber.\n\n[patient] Alright, I understand.\n\n[doctor] We\'ll discuss all the details further, but I\'ll summarize: the surgery will take about an hour and a half, you\'ll spend the night in the hospital, and you should avoid high-fat foods and eat high-fiber foods after surgery.\n\n[patient] Got it.\n\n[doctor] Also, given your type 2 diabetes, you should refrain from hiking in the immediate postoperative period. However, you may return to walking on solid ground 1 week after surgery.\n\n[patient] I see.\n\n[doctor] We\'ll continue to monitor your diabetes as outlined by your endocrinologist or primary care provider.\n\n[patient] Okay, that sounds good.\n\n[doctor] Do you have any questions or concerns about the surgery or the treatment plan?\n\n[patient] No, I think I understand everything.\n\n[doctor] Great. You\'ll follow up with me in 1 week after the surgery. Do you have any questions about that?\n\n[patient] No, that\'s fine.\n\n[doctor] Alright, I\'ll see you in 1 week. Is there anything else you\'d like to add or ask?\n\n[patient] No, that\'s all.\n\n[doctor] Okay, I\'ll see you then. Take care and feel better.\n\n[patient] Thank you, doctor.\n\n[doctor] You\'re welcome. Take care. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Doctor: Hello. Hello. Hi. Hello. Hello. Hello. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. 