[92mlitellm.acompletion(model='gpt-4o', api_base=None, messages=[{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a fair judge assistant assigned to deliver insightful feedback that compares individual performances, highlighting how each stands relative to others within the same cohort.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of two responses strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, choose a better response between Response A and Response B. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: "(write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (A or B)"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###Instruction:\n\nImagine you are a medical professional tasked with evaluating simulated doctor-patient conversations generated from summary notes. \nThese conversations should accurately reconstruct interactions based on the SOAP (Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan) format provided in the notes.\n\nHere is the medical note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight shoulder injury.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMatthew Allen is a 67-year-old male, right-hand-dominant, male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of a right shoulder injury. The patient sustained this injury 4 days ago, when he was tackled a couple of times playing pick up football and landed on his right shoulder. He states that he was able to return to the game, however he has had pain since that time. The patient locates his pain to the posterior aspect of his shoulder. He denies any numbness or tingling in his fingers. He states that the strength in his hand is okay; however, he has been trying to avoid lifting anything too heavy. Mr. Allen denies being able to touch his shoulder to his ear because of pain. The patient has been taking Tylenol for pain, which does not provide relief. He denies any neck problems or other joint problems. The patient denies any previous orthopedic injuries to his right shoulder. He denies any fever or chills since the injury, but does note a headache. The patient states that he hit his head at the time of the injury but did not pass out.\n\nSURGICAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient has a history of an appendectomy 3 to 4 years ago.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fever or chills.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right shoulder pain. Denies neck pain.\nNeurological: Reports headache. Denies numbness or tingling.\n\nVITALS\n\nBP: 122/76.\nHR: 82 bpm.\nSpO2: 95% on room air.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNECK: Examination of the cervical spine reveals no tenderness to palpation. Full ROM.\nHEAD: There does not appear to be any trauma. No ecchymosis or bruising.\nMSK: Examination of the right shoulder reveals pain to palpation of the posterior shoulder at the acromioclavicular joint. There is decreased abduction and adduction to about 90 degrees only. Pain with supination and pronation of the right arm. Palpable radial artery pulse.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right shoulder reveals an acute acromioclavicular joint fracture.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight shoulder acute acromioclavicular joint fracture.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I have explained to him that his x-rays revealed an acute acromioclavicular joint fracture. The plan is to place the patient in a sling and prescribe meloxicam 15 mg once a day to treat his pain. I also recommend that we obtain a CT scan of the right shoulder to evaluate further. I recommend that the patient attend formal physical therapy to strengthen his right shoulder in hopes of avoiding surgery in the future. In the meantime, I have advised him to avoid tackle football.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor] Hi there, how are you doing today?\n\n[patient] Not too well, I\'ve been having a lot of pain in my right shoulder.\n\n[doctor] I see. Can you tell me more about what\'s been going on?\n\n[patient] Sure. I was playing football a few days ago and got tackled a couple of times. I landed on my right shoulder and ever since then, I\'ve had a lot of pain.\n\n[doctor] Hmm, that sounds like it could be quite painful. Did you manage to continue playing after the injury?\n\n[patient] Yeah, I did, but the pain has been there ever since.\n\n[doctor] I see. Where exactly do you feel the pain?\n\n[patient] It\'s mainly on the back of my shoulder.\n\n[doctor] Okay. Do you feel any numbness or tingling in your fingers?\n\n[patient] No, I don\'t feel any numbness or tingling in my fingers.\n\n[doctor] Alright. How about the strength in your hand? Is that okay?\n\n[patient] It\'s okay, but I\'ve been trying to avoid lifting anything too heavy because of the pain.\n\n[doctor] I understand. Can you touch your shoulder to your ear?\n\n[patient] No, that\'s too painful.\n\n[doctor] Okay. Have you been taking any pain medication?\n\n[patient] Yes, I\'ve been taking Tylenol, but it doesn\'t really help much.\n\n[doctor] Hmm, I see. Do you have any neck problems or other joint problems?\n\n[patient] No, I don\'t have any neck problems or other joint problems.\n\n[doctor] Have you had any previous orthopedic injuries to your right shoulder?\n\n[patient] No, I haven\'t had any previous injuries to my right shoulder.\n\n[doctor] Okay. Have you had any fever or chills since the injury?\n\n[patient] No, I haven\'t had any fever or chills.\n\n[doctor] Do you have any headache?\n\n[patient] Yes, I\'ve had a headache.\n\n[doctor] Hmm, okay. Did you hit your head at the time of the injury?\n\n[patient] Yes, I hit my head when I fell, but I didn\'t pass out.\n\n[doctor] Alright. Let\'s review your medical history. You had an appendectomy about 3 to 4 years ago, is that correct?\n\n[patient] Yes, that\'s right.\n\n[doctor] Okay. How about your overall health? Any fever or chills?\n\n[patient] No, I haven\'t had any fever or chills.\n\n[doctor] Any other symptoms?\n\n[patient] Just the pain in my shoulder.\n\n[doctor] Alright, let\'s check your vital signs. Your blood pressure is 122/76, your heart rate is 82 bpm, and your oxygen saturation is 95% on room air. Everything looks good there.\n\n[patient] Okay.\n\n[doctor] Now, let\'s do a physical exam. I\'ll start with your neck. Is there any tenderness to palpation?\n\n[patient] No, my neck doesn\'t hurt.\n\n[doctor] Okay. Let\'s move on to your head. Do you see any trauma or bruising?\n\n[patient] No, there\'s no trauma or bruising.\n\n[doctor] Alright. Now, let\'s examine your right shoulder. I\'ll check for any pain to palpation. Is there any pain on the back of your shoulder?\n\n[patient] Yes, there is.\n\n[doctor] Hmm. How about abduction and adduction of your right arm? Can you move it?\n\n[patient] It\'s limited, I can only move it about 90 degrees.\n\n[doctor] Okay. What about supination and pronation of your right arm? Is there any pain there?\n\n[patient] Yes, there is pain.\n\n[doctor] Alright. I can feel your radial artery pulse. It\'s normal. Now, let\'s do a quick X-ray of your right shoulder. The results show an acute acromioclavicular joint fracture.\n\n[patient] Oh no, what does that mean?\n\n[doctor] It means you have a fracture in the joint between your shoulder blade and collarbone. We\'ll need to do a CT scan to evaluate it further.\n\n[patient] Okay, what\'s the plan?\n\n[doctor] We\'ll put you in a sling to help immobilize your shoulder and reduce the pain. I\'ll also prescribe meloxicam 15 mg once a day to help with pain. Additionally, I recommend you start formal physical therapy to strengthen your right shoulder. This will help you avoid surgery in the future.\n\n[patient] Alright, that sounds good.\n\n[doctor] Also, I advise you to avoid playing tackle football for now. It\'s best to give your shoulder time to heal.\n\n[patient] Okay, I\'ll stop playing football.\n\n[doctor] Great. We\'ll follow up with the CT scan and physical therapy. How do you feel about all of this?\n\n[patient] I\'m a bit worried, but I\'ll do what I need to do.\n\n[doctor] That\'s a good attitude. We\'ll get you all fixed up and back on the road to recovery. Any questions or concerns?\n\n[patient] No, I think I understand.\n\n[doctor] Alright, I\'ll see you in a few days for the CT scan and we\'ll go from there. Take care and see you soon.\n\n[patient] Thank you, doctor. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Doctor: Hi there. Can you hear me okay? Can you hear me okay? Great. So, hi. Can you tell me a little bit about what\'s been going on with your shoulder, please?\n\nPatient: Yeah, so I\'ve been having some pain in my shoulder for a few days now. I think I hurt it a few days ago playing football.\n\nDoctor: Okay. So, how long ago was that?\n\nPatient: Um, I think it was about four days ago.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And how did you hurt it?\n\nPatient: I was playing football and I got tackled a couple of times and I landed on my shoulder.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And what\'s been going on since then? Have you been able to move your shoulder at all? Have you been able to lift anything?\n\nPatient: Yeah, I\'ve been trying to avoid lifting anything too heavy. I haven\'t been able to touch my shoulder to my ear because of the pain.\n\nDoctor: Okay. Have you been able to move your arm at all? Have you been able to move your shoulder at all?\n\nPatient: Yeah, I\'ve been able to move it a little bit, but it\'s been really painful.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And have you been able to use your arm to lift anything at all? Have you been able to lift your arm up and down?\n\nPatient: Yeah, I\'ve been able to lift it up and down a little bit, but it\'s been really painful.\n\nDoctor: Okay. Have you been a