-d '{'model': 'gpt-4o', 'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a fair judge assistant assigned to deliver insightful feedback that compares individual performances, highlighting how each stands relative to others within the same cohort.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of two responses strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, choose a better response between Response A and Response B. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: "(write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (A or B)"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###Instruction:\n\nImagine you are a medical professional tasked with evaluating simulated doctor-patient conversations generated from summary notes. \nThese conversations should accurately reconstruct interactions based on the SOAP (Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan) format provided in the notes.\n\nHere is the medical note:\n#############################\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJeremy Adams is a pleasant 57-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of low back pain. The onset of his pain began 4 months ago, when he was moving boxes out of his son’s college dorm. At that time, he noted tingling in his right toes, which resolved after approximately 1 hour. After his injury, he felt as though he was unable to support his upper body weight. The patient reports weakness in his legs when carrying heavy objects. He reports a sharp pain in his lower back when he turns a certain way or rolls over in his sleep. The pain will resolve with repositioning. The patient was seen at urgent care and was referred to obtain an MRI, however he did not undergo this secondary to insurance issues. He has been utilizing Tylenol, ibuprofen, heat, and ice for pain, which does not provide relief. The patient notes the ibuprofen is slightly more beneficial than the Tylenol, however it does not relieve his symptoms. He denies upset stomach with the use of ibuprofen. Additionally, he attempted physical therapy at his gym without any relief. He denies any bladder or bowels issues, fevers, and chills.\n\nSURGICAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient has a history of a right total knee arthroplasty 2 years ago, in spring of 2020. He states that his right knee feels 95% improved since the surgery.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers or chills.\nGastrointestinal: Denies bowel issues.\nGenitourinary: Denies bladder issues.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports lower back pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nTemperature: Normal.\nBlood pressure: 117/56 mm Hg.\nHeart rate: 68 BPM.\nOxygenation: 95% on room air.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNECK: Bony protuberance of the C5-C6. No pain to palpation of the C5-C6\nMSK: Examination of the Lumbar spine: No pain to palpation of the lumbar spine. Decreased flexion and extension of the lower back. Positive straight leg raise. Spasm of the paraspinal muscles of the lumbar spine. Strength is equal bilaterally.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the lumbar spine were reviewed. These reveal no acute bony abnormalities.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nLow back pain, possible acute disc herniation.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. His x-rays did not reveal any signs of a fracture. I would like to obtain an MRI of the lumbar spine to evaluate for a possible acute disc herniation. I have prescribed the patient meloxicam 15 mg to treat his pain. He will continue with physical therapy and a referral was provided for this today.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor]: Hi Jeremy, how are you doing today?\n\n[patient]: Hi, I\'m okay. Thanks for seeing me.\n\n[doctor]: Great, I\'m glad to see you. What brings you in today?\n\n[patient]: I\'ve been having some pretty bad back pain.\n\n[doctor]: Ah, sorry to hear that. Can you tell me more about your pain?\n\n[patient]: It started about four months ago when I was moving some boxes out of my son\'s college dorm. I felt a tingling in my right toes, which went away after about an hour. But after that, I couldn\'t support my upper body weight like I used to.\n\n[doctor]: Hmm, that sounds quite uncomfortable. Have you noticed any other symptoms?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I have weakness in my legs when I try to carry heavy objects. It\'s like I\'m not as strong as I used to be.\n\n[doctor]: I see. How about at night, do you experience any pain when you roll over or turn in your sleep?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I do. It\'s a sharp pain in my lower back. But if I reposition myself, it goes away.\n\n[doctor]: Okay, got it. Have you tried any treatments for the pain so far?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I\'ve been using Tylenol and ibuprofen. I\'ve also tried heat and ice. The ibuprofen seems to be a bit more helpful than the Tylenol, but it doesn\'t really relieve the pain.\n\n[doctor]: Hmm, interesting. Have you noticed any stomach upset from taking ibuprofen?\n\n[patient]: No, I haven\'t had any stomach issues.\n\n[doctor]: Good to know. Have you tried physical therapy at all?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I went to physical therapy at my gym, but it didn\'t help.\n\n[doctor]: Okay. Any other symptoms like fever, chills, or changes in bowel or bladder habits?\n\n[patient]: No, none of those.\n\n[doctor]: Alright, let\'s review your medical history. You had a right total knee arthroplasty about two years ago, in the spring of 2020. How\'s your knee doing now?\n\n[patient]: It\'s 95% better since the surgery.\n\n[doctor]: That\'s great to hear. Let\'s check your vital signs. Your temperature is normal, blood pressure is 117/56 mm Hg, heart rate is 68 BPM, and oxygenation is 95% on room air. All good.\n\n[doctor]: Now, I\'ll do a physical examination. I\'ll start with your neck. I\'ll check for any pain or tenderness. There is a bony protuberance at the C5-C6 area, but it doesn\'t hurt when I press on it.\n\n[patient]: Okay.\n\n[doctor]: Next, I\'ll examine your musculoskeletal system. I\'ll check your lumbar spine. There\'s no pain when I press on it, but you have decreased flexion and extension of the lower back. I\'ll also check for any muscle spasm. I do feel some spasm in the paraspinal muscles of the lumbar spine. Your strength is equal bilaterally.\n\n[patient]: Alright.\n\n[doctor]: Now, I\'ll review the results of your x-rays. They didn\'t show any acute bony abnormalities.\n\n[patient]: That\'s good to know.\n\n[doctor]: Based on your symptoms and the physical examination, it\'s possible that you may have an acute disc herniation. I\'d like to get an MRI of your lumbar spine to confirm this.\n\n[patient]: Okay, that sounds good.\n\n[doctor]: I\'ll prescribe you meloxicam 15 mg to help manage your pain. I\'ll also refer you to physical therapy to help with your back pain. Do you have any questions about this?\n\n[patient]: No, that sounds good. Thank you.\n\n[doctor]: Great. I\'ll get the MRI scheduled for you. We\'ll go over the results once we have them. Do you have any other questions?\n\n[patient]: No, that\'s all for now.\n\n[doctor]: Alright, I\'ll see you in a few weeks to follow up and discuss the MRI results. Take care and feel better.\n\n[patient]: Thank you, I\'ll see you then. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Doctor: Hi there, welcome to the clinic. My name is Doctor Smith. How are you doing today?\n\nPatient: Hi, I\'m fine. Thank you.\n\nDoctor: Good. Can you tell me a bit more about what\'s been going on with your back?\n\nPatient: Yeah, so I\'ve been having some lower back pain, and it started a few months ago. And I\'ve been trying to treat it with ibuprofen and heat and ice, but it\'s not really been helping.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And when did the pain start?\n\nPatient: Um, I think it started about four months ago. I was moving some boxes out of my son\'s college dorm and I twisted and turned and felt a sharp pain in my lower back.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And how has the pain been since then? Has it been constant or has it come and gone?\n\nPatient: It\'s been pretty consistent. It\'s been pretty much every day. Sometimes it\'s a dull ache, sometimes it\'s a sharp pain. And sometimes it\'s more sore when I move certain ways.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And what kind of movements make it worse?\n\nPatient: Um, rolling over in my sleep, twisting, bending. Any kind of movement that involves twisting or bending really makes it worse.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And how would you rate the pain on a scale of one to ten, one being very mild and ten being very severe?\n\nPatient: I would say it\'s probably around a seven.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And have you noticed any numbness or tingling in your legs?\n\nPatient: Yeah, when I first started having the pain, I had tingling in my toes, but that went away after a few days.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And have you noticed any weakness in your legs at all?\n\nPatient: Yeah, when I\'m carrying heavy objects, I can\'t lift them as well as I used to. And if I\'m bending down to pick something up, I can feel the strain in my lower back.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And have you noticed any numbness or tingling in your feet at all when you\'re walking or standing?\n\nPatient: Yeah, sometimes when I\'m standing for a long time, I can feel some numbness in my toes.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And have you noticed any weakness in your legs when you\'re standing or walking?\n\nPatient: Yeah, when I\'m walking, I have to hold onto things to help me balance, and sometimes I trip and fall.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And have you noticed any numbness or tingling in your feet when you\'re sitting?\n\nPatient: Yeah, sometimes when I\'m sitting, I can feel some numbness in my toes.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And have you noticed any weakness in your legs when you\'re sitting?\n\nPatient: Yeah, sometimes when I\'m sitting, I have to use my arms to help me stand up.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And have you noticed any weakness in your legs when you\'re lying down?\n\nPatient: Yeah, sometimes when I\'m lying down, I have to use my arms to help me roll over.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And have you noticed any numbness or tingling in your feet when you\'re lying down?\n\nPatient: Yeah, sometimes when I\'m lying down, I can feel some numbness in my toes.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And have you noticed any weakness in your legs when you\'re lying down?\n\nPatient: Yeah, sometimes when I\'m lying down, I have to use my arms to help me roll over.\n\nDoctor: Okay. Have you noticed any numbness or tingling in your feet when you\'re walking?\n\nPatient: Yeah, sometimes when I\'m walking, I can feel some numbness in my toes.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And have you noticed any weakness in your legs when you\'re walking?\n\nPatient: Yeah, sometimes when I\'m walking, I have to hold onto things to help me balance, and sometimes I trip and fall.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And have you noticed any weakness in your legs when you\'re standing?\n\nPatient: Yeah, sometimes when I\'m standing, I have to hold onto things to help me balance, and sometimes I trip and fall.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And have you noticed any numbness or tingling in your feet when you\'re standing?\n\nPatient: Yeah, sometimes when I\'m standing, I can feel some numbness in my toes.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And have you noticed any weakness in your legs when you\'re standing?\n\nPatient: Yeah, sometimes when I\'m standing, I have to hold onto things to help me balance, and sometimes I trip and fall.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And have you noticed any numbness or tingling in your feet when you\'re lying down?\n\nPatient: Yeah, sometimes when I\'m lying down, I can feel some numbness in my toes.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And have you noticed any weakness in your legs when you\'re lying down?\n\nPatient: Yeah, sometimes whe