[92mlitellm.acompletion(model='gpt-4o', api_base=None, messages=[{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a fair judge assistant assigned to deliver insightful feedback that compares individual performances, highlighting how each stands relative to others within the same cohort.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of two responses strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, choose a better response between Response A and Response B. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: "(write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (A or B)"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###Instruction:\n\nImagine you are a medical professional tasked with evaluating simulated doctor-patient conversations generated from summary notes. \nThese conversations should accurately reconstruct interactions based on the SOAP (Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan) format provided in the notes.\n\nHere is the medical note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight elbow pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nTeresa Sullivan is a pleasant 33-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right elbow pain. Her pain began 4 days ago, without any improvement. She denies any specific injury; however, she has recently increased her usage of a pottery wheel in creating a bowl for her son. She is currently unable to work with the pottery wheel secondary to pain. Her pain is in the medial aspect of her elbow. She notes it radiates medially down her arm as well. The patient rates her pain level as 7 out of 10 at its worst. She reports the pain wakes her at night if she rolls over. She denies ibuprofen or ice provide pain relief. The patient denies any previous elbow injuries.\n\nThe patient has a history of hypertension. She states she has been checking her blood pressures regularly. She adds that her blood pressures have been in the 110s to 130s range. She affirms she is taking lisinopril 10 mg daily and denies missing doses.\n\nRegarding her diabetes type 2, she reports she sometimes forgets to take metformin 500 mg twice daily. At times, her blood sugar levels range from 150 to 170 mg/dL. She states that she has a sweet tooth and indulges in home baked goods. For exercise, she walks in the evening and in the morning. She walks 1 to 2 miles per day around her neighborhood.\n\nThe patient enjoys working with ceramics as well as baking cakes and cinnamon rolls for family gatherings.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of hypertension and diabetes type 2.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking lisinopril 10 mg and metformin 500 mg twice daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood Pressure: 120/70 mmHg.\nPulse Rate: Appropriate.\nRespiratory Rate: 18 breaths per minute.\nOxygen Saturation: 100% on room air.\nBody Temperature: 97.9 degrees F.\nPain Level: 7/10 at its worst.\nWeight: 5 lb increase since last visit.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: No murmurs, gallops or rubs. Pulses are equal bilaterally.\nRESPIRATORY: Normal respiratory effort, no respiratory distress. Lungs are clear bilaterally to auscultation.\nSKIN: No rash or lesions.\nMSK: Examination of the right elbow: Moderate tenderness at the medial epicondyle. Pain with resisted pronation of the right forearm. Pain with flexion and bending the right wrist. No bruising or swelling. No discoloration or lesions.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAn x-ray of the right elbow was reviewed today. It demonstrated no evidence of any fractures. No other abnormalities are noted.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Right elbow pain, consistent with medial epicondylitis.\n2. Diabetes type 2.\n3. Hypertension.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with her regarding her current symptoms. I have explained to her that her elbow pain is consistent with medial epicondylitis and was most likely caused by overuse and damage to the tendons in her arm. I have recommended that we treat the patient conservatively. The patient will wear a sling on her right arm while she is awake to provide increased support. I advised her to ice her right elbow for 20 minutes 3 times per day. She will take ibuprofen 600 mg every 6 hours with food for 1 week. I have also advised her to hold off on creating pottery for the next 2 weeks to allow her arm to heal. If the patient continues to experience persistent elbow pain, we may consider formal physical therapy at her next visit.\n\nI have encouraged the patient to check and record her blood sugars every day. I will increase her metformin to 1000 mg twice a day. The patient and I discussed the importance of her taking her medication on a regular basis twice a day. I have also recommended that she be referred to diabetic counseling for more information on the steps she can take to manage her diabetes. I will also order a hemoglobin A1c test for a more accurate assessment of her long-term blood sugar levels.\n\nThe patient’s hypertension is currently controlled with medication and exercise. I have instructed her to continue taking lisinopril 10 mg. I also encouraged her to continue with her walking routine.\n\nAll questions were answered.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks to review the results of the hemoglobin A1c test and discuss her progress.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor]: Hi there, how are you doing today?\n\n[patient]: Hi, I\'m not so great. I\'ve been having a lot of pain in my right elbow.\n\n[doctor]: Oh, sorry to hear that. Can you tell me more about the pain?\n\n[patient]: It started four days ago, and it hasn\'t gotten any better. I\'ve been using a pottery wheel to make a bowl for my son, and I think that might be the cause.\n\n[doctor]: Ah, that sounds like it could be a good clue. Where exactly is the pain located?\n\n[patient]: It\'s on the medial side of my elbow.\n\n[doctor]: Does the pain radiate down your arm?\n\n[patient]: Yes, it does. It goes down my arm, medially.\n\n[doctor]: That can be quite uncomfortable. How would you rate your pain on a scale of 1 to 10?\n\n[patient]: It\'s about a 7 at its worst.\n\n[doctor]: Okay, got it. Does the pain wake you up at night?\n\n[patient]: Yes, it does. If I roll over, it wakes me up.\n\n[doctor]: Hmm, that can be quite bothersome. Have you tried using ibuprofen or applying ice to see if it helps?\n\n[patient]: No, I haven\'t tried either of those.\n\n[doctor]: Alright, we\'ll try those next. Have you had any previous elbow injuries?\n\n[patient]: No, none that I know of.\n\n[doctor]: Okay, good. Do you have any other medical conditions?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I have hypertension and diabetes type 2.\n\n[doctor]: How have you been managing those?\n\n[patient]: I take lisinopril 10 mg daily and metformin 500 mg twice daily. I usually check my blood pressure regularly, and it\'s been in the 110s to 130s range.\n\n[doctor]: That\'s good to hear. How often do you forget to take your metformin?\n\n[patient]: Sometimes I forget to take it, especially when I\'m busy.\n\n[doctor]: I see. How do you manage your blood sugar levels?\n\n[patient]: They usually range from 150 to 170 mg/dL.\n\n[doctor]: That\'s important to keep an eye on. What kind of exercise do you do?\n\n[patient]: I walk 1 to 2 miles per day around my neighborhood in the morning and evening.\n\n[doctor]: Good exercise routine. Do you have any hobbies?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I enjoy working with ceramics and baking cakes and cinnamon rolls for family gatherings.\n\n[doctor]: That sounds like fun. Let me review your medical history. You have hypertension and diabetes type 2.\n\n[patient]: Yes, that\'s right.\n\n[doctor]: You\'re currently taking lisinopril 10 mg and metformin 500 mg twice daily.\n\n[patient]: Yes, that\'s correct.\n\n[doctor]: Let\'s go over your vital signs. Your blood pressure is 120/70 mmHg, your pulse rate is appropriate, your respiratory rate is 18 breaths per minute, your oxygen saturation is 100% on room air, and your body temperature is 97.9 degrees Fahrenheit. You\'ve gained 5 pounds since your last visit.\n\n[patient]: Okay.\n\n[doctor]: Now, let\'s do a physical exam. I\'ll check your cardiovascular system, respiratory system, and skin. I\'ll also examine your musculoskeletal system. Your heart sounds are normal, no murmurs, gallops, or rubs. Your pulses are equal bilaterally. Your lungs are clear bilaterally to auscultation. Your skin is normal, no rash or lesions. \n\n[patient]: Okay.\n\n[doctor]: Now, let\'s examine your right elbow. There is moderate tenderness at the medial epicondyle. You experience pain when I resist pronation of your right forearm and when you flex and bend your right wrist. There\'s no bruising or swelling, no discoloration or lesions.\n\n[patient]: That hurts a bit.\n\n[doctor]: Alright, I\'m going to order an x-ray of your right elbow. Let me review the results. The x-ray shows no evidence of any fractures, and there are no other abnormalities noted.\n\n[patient]: Okay.\n\n[doctor]: Based on your symptoms and the exam, it appears that you have medial epicondylitis, which is inflammation of the tendons in your arm. I think this was caused by overuse and damage from using the pottery wheel.\n\n[patient]: Oh, I see.\n\n[doctor]: I\'m going to recommend a conservative treatment approach. You\'ll need to wear a sling on your right arm while you\'re awake to provide support. You\'ll also apply ice to your right elbow for 20 minutes, three times a day. Take ibuprofen 600 mg every 6 hours with food for one week. It\'s also important that you avoid using the pottery wheel for the next two weeks to allow your arm to heal.\n\n[patient]: Okay, I\'ll do that.\n\n[doctor]: I\'ve also increased your metformin to 1000 mg twice a day to help with your diabetes. It\'s crucial that you take your medication regularly, twice a day. We\'ll also schedule a hemoglobin A1c test to get a more accurate assessment of your blood sugar levels.\n\n[patient]: Alright.\n\n[doctor]: Your hypertension is currently under control with your current medication and exercise. Continue taking lisinopril 10 mg and keep up with your walking routine.\n\n[patient]: Okay, I\'ll keep doing that.\n\n[doctor]: Do you have any questions or concerns?\n\n[patient]: No, I think you\'ve covered everything.\n\n[doctor]: Great. We\'ll schedule a follow-up in two weeks to review the results of the hemoglobin A1c test and discuss your progress. Do you have any other questions?\n\n[patient]: No, that\'s all.\n\n[doctor]: Alright, I\'ll see you in two weeks. Take care and get well soon.\n\n[patient]: Thank you, doctor. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Doctor: Hi there. Can you just confirm your name and date of birth, please?\nPatient: Sure. My name is Teresa Sullivan. And my date of birth is September 14, 1988.\nDoctor: Okay, Teresa. So can you confirm your address?\nPatient: Yes. My address is 123 Main Street, Anytown, USA.\nDoctor: Okay. And can you confirm your phone number?\nPatient: Yes. My phone number is 555-123-4567.\nDoctor: Okay. Okay. And do you have any other medical problems at the moment?\nPatient: Um, I have hypertension. And I have diabetes. Um, I\'m not sure what my blood sugar levels are, but I\'m supposed to be checking them regularly. And I\'m supposed to be taking metformin.\nDoctor: Okay. And do you take any other medications?\nPatient: Yes. I take lisinopril.\nDoctor: Okay. And how often do you take it?\nPatient: Um, I take it in the morning and at night.\nDoctor: Okay. And what\'s the dose?\nPatient: Um, I think it\'s 10 milligrams.\nDoctor: Okay. And do you ta