[92mlitellm.acompletion(model='gpt-4o', api_base=None, messages=[{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a fair judge assistant assigned to deliver insightful feedback that compares individual performances, highlighting how each stands relative to others within the same cohort.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of two responses strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, choose a better response between Response A and Response B. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: "(write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (A or B)"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###Instruction:\n\nImagine you are a medical professional tasked with evaluating simulated doctor-patient conversations generated from summary notes. \nThese conversations should accurately reconstruct interactions based on the SOAP (Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan) format provided in the notes.\n\nHere is the medical note:\n#############################\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nGregory Price is a pleasant 68-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of a right knee injury. The onset of his pain began 2.5 weeks ago, when he stepped off of a curb and turned over his ankle. He locates his pain to the lateral aspect of his right knee. The patient also reports clicking with ambulation. He denies any pain with ambulation unless he stands on one foot for an extended period of time. The patient also reports locking of his knee with prolonged standing. He notices pain when he is resting his foot on his heel. He denies any pain at rest and elevated, or when his foot is flat on the ground. The patient states that he is more active during the day, which does not impact his knee pain. He adds that he is able to perform a full squat without pain. The patient reports numbness and tingling in his right foot, however this is not new. The patient has been taking ibuprofen, which provides him with relief at night. He has also been icing his knee 4 times per day, which provides him with relief in the morning and at the end of the day.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nHe states that he has fractured his 2nd toe 3 times over the last 10 years.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient works in sales. He rides a bike for exercise.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nNeurological: Positive for numbness and tingling in his toes on his right foot from prior injury.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: >Palpable dorsalis pedis and posterior tibialis pulse.\nMSK: Examination of the right knee: Slight edema. Slight effusion. No pain with palpation of the right lateral knee. Positive varus test. Full ROM. Negative Lachman\'s.\n\nRESULTS\n\n4 views of the right knee were taken. These reveal no evidence of any fractures, dislocations, or bony abnormality. No other abnormalities are noted.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight knee lateral collateral ligament strain.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I have explained to him that his x-rays did not reveal any acute fractures or bony abnormality. I have recommended that we treat the patient conservatively with formal physical therapy to strengthen his right knee. I have also prescribed the patient meloxicam 15 mg to treat his pain. If his pain does not improve with physical therapy, I will recommend obtaining an MRI.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor] Good morning, how are you today?\n\n[patient] Good morning, doctor. I\'m doing okay, thanks. I\'ve been having some issues with my knee lately.\n\n[doctor] Ah, I\'m glad you\'re here. What seems to be the problem with your knee?\n\n[patient] Well, about two and a half weeks ago, I stepped off a curb and twisted my ankle. Since then, I\'ve been having a lot of pain on the side of my right knee.\n\n[doctor] Hmm, I see. Can you show me exactly where the pain is?\n\n[patient] Yes, it\'s right here. On the lateral side.\n\n[doctor] Okay. Do you notice any other symptoms, like clicking or anything else?\n\n[patient] Yes, when I walk, I can hear a clicking sound. But it doesn\'t hurt when I\'m walking unless I stand on one foot for a long time.\n\n[doctor] Got it. And how about when you stand for a long time, do you feel any pain then?\n\n[patient] Yes, my knee locks up when I\'m standing for a while. And if I rest my foot on my heel, I feel pain.\n\n[doctor] Okay. How about when you\'re at rest or when your foot is flat on the ground, is there any pain then?\n\n[patient] No, there isn\'t.\n\n[doctor] Alright. How has your activity level been? Has it affected your daily activities?\n\n[patient] I\'m actually more active during the day, but it doesn\'t seem to affect the pain.\n\n[doctor] That\'s good to know. Can you do a full squat without pain?\n\n[patient] Yes, I can do a full squat without any pain.\n\n[doctor] And do you have any numbness or tingling in your right foot?\n\n[patient] Yes, I have numbness and tingling in my right foot, but it\'s not new. It\'s been there since the ankle injury.\n\n[doctor] Alright. Have you been trying any treatments for the pain?\n\n[patient] Yes, I\'ve been taking ibuprofen at night, and it helps with the pain. I\'ve also been icing my knee four times a day, which helps in the morning and at the end of the day.\n\n[doctor] Okay. Let\'s go over your medical history. Have you had any other injuries or illnesses?\n\n[patient] I\'ve fractured my second toe three times over the last 10 years.\n\n[doctor] I see. Do you work?\n\n[patient] Yes, I work in sales.\n\n[doctor] And do you have any exercise habits?\n\n[patient] I ride a bike for exercise.\n\n[doctor] Alright. Let\'s do a quick review of your systems. How\'s your neurological system been?\n\n[patient] I have numbness and tingling in my toes on my right foot from the ankle injury.\n\n[doctor] Okay. Any other issues?\n\n[patient] No, nothing else.\n\n[doctor] Let\'s do a physical exam. I\'ll check your cardiovascular system first. Do you have any issues with your heart or blood circulation?\n\n[patient] No, none that I know of.\n\n[doctor] Okay. Now, I\'ll check your musculoskeletal system. I\'ll start with your right knee. I\'ll check for any swelling or fluid accumulation. I\'ll also check for pain when I press on different areas. I\'ll also perform some special tests like the varus test.\n\n[patient] Okay.\n\n[doctor] Let\'s start with the examination. There\'s some slight edema and a slight effusion in your right knee. There\'s no pain when I press on the lateral aspect. The varus test is positive, indicating a possible ligament strain. Your range of motion is full, and the Lachman\'s test is negative.\n\n[patient] Okay.\n\n[doctor] Now, I\'ll order some x-rays of your right knee to rule out any fractures or bony abnormalities.\n\n[patient] Alright.\n\n[doctor] The results of the x-rays show no evidence of any fractures, dislocations, or bony abnormalities. Based on the examination and the x-ray results, I suspect a right knee lateral collateral ligament strain.\n\n[patient] A ligament strain?\n\n[doctor] Yes, it\'s a strain in the ligament on the outside of your knee. We\'ll treat it conservatively with formal physical therapy to strengthen your right knee. I\'ll also prescribe meloxicam 15 mg to help with the pain. If your pain doesn\'t improve with physical therapy, we\'ll consider getting an MRI.\n\n[patient] Okay, that sounds good. What\'s the plan for the physical therapy?\n\n[doctor] We\'ll schedule an appointment with our physical therapist, who will work with you to develop a personalized exercise plan to strengthen your knee. We\'ll also monitor your progress and adjust the plan as needed.\n\n[patient] Alright, that sounds good. When do I start the physical therapy?\n\n[doctor] We\'ll get you scheduled for an appointment within the next week. We\'ll also start you on the meloxicam today. Take one tablet once a day with food. If you have any questions or concerns, feel free to reach out to us.\n\n[patient] Alright, thank you.\n\n[doctor] You\'re welcome. We\'ll see how you progress with the physical therapy and adjust the treatment plan accordingly. Take care.\n\n[patient] Thank you, doctor. I\'ll see you soon.\n\n[doctor] See you soon. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Doctor: Hello, good morning. Can you confirm your name and date of birth for me, please? \nPatient: Gregory Price. November 13th, 1952. \nDoctor: OK, great. OK, OK. So, good morning. So, can you just confirm your name and date of birth for me, please?\nPatient: Gregory Price. November 13th, 1952.\nDoctor: OK, great. OK. And can you confirm your address for me, please?\nPatient: Uh, 123 Main Street.\nDoctor: OK, great. OK. And can you confirm your contact details for me, please?\nPatient: Uh, 123 Main Street. 555-555-5555.\nDoctor: OK, great. OK. And can you confirm your next of kin\'s name and contact details for me, please?\nPatient: Uh, my wife, Sarah. Uh, 123 Main Street. 555-555-5555.\nDoctor: OK, great. OK. So, what can I do for you today?\nPatient: Uh, I have a knee problem.\nDoctor: OK, great. OK. Can you just tell me a little bit more about what\'s been going on with your knee?\nPatient: Uh, OK. Uh, so I, I stepped off a curb, and I twisted my ankle, and I\'ve had pain in my knee ever since. And it\'s, it\'s been getting progressively worse over time.\nDoctor: OK, great. OK. And can you tell me where you\'re feeling the pain in your knee?\nPatient: Uh, it\'s on the side of my knee.\nDoctor: OK, great. And can you just show me where you\'re feeling the pain, if you can?\nPatient: Uh, OK. Uh, yeah.\nDoctor: OK. And ca