-d '{'model': 'gpt-4o', 'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a fair judge assistant assigned to deliver insightful feedback that compares individual performances, highlighting how each stands relative to others within the same cohort.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of two responses strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, choose a better response between Response A and Response B. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: "(write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (A or B)"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###Instruction:\n\nImagine you are a medical professional tasked with evaluating simulated doctor-patient conversations generated from summary notes. \nThese conversations should accurately reconstruct interactions based on the SOAP (Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan) format provided in the notes.\n\nHere is the medical note:\n#############################\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nDennis Martin is a pleasant 46-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right heel pain. The onset of his pain began 10 days ago while training for a triathlon. He locates his pain to the posterior aspect of his heel, which radiates proximally into his calf. His pain is aggravated by working out and when he first gets up in the morning. If he sits for a long period, his pain will return upon standing. The patient states that he runs 10 kilometers in the morning. At lunchtime, he tries to get on a bike for about 45 minutes to an hour. In the evening, he goes to the aquatic center and swims for 30 minutes to 1 hour. The patient states that he runs 5 days per week. He adds that he alternates between a long run and a long bike ride on weekends. He currently takes one day off from his exercises. The patient states that he does not stretch at all. He denies any left leg pain.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right heel pain. Denies left leg pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: Examination of the right heel: Pain with resisted extension. Mild pain with flexion. Positive calcaneal squeeze test. Severe tenderness to the Achilles tendon. Thompson test is negative, bilaterally.\n\nRESULTS\n\nNo new imaging was obtained today\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight Achilles tendinitis.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination today, I had a lengthy discussion with the patient regarding his current symptoms. I have explained to him that his symptoms are likely secondary to overuse. I have recommended that we treat the patient conservatively. The patient was instructed to take at least 1 week off from his training to recover from this. I will provide him with a referral for formal physical therapy to strengthen his right lower extremities. I have advised him to take 3 tablets of ibuprofen every 6 hours for pain. If his pain does not improve with physical therapy, I will recommend obtaining an x-ray.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 7 to 10 days to check on his progress.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor]: Hi there, how are you doing today?\n\n[patient]: Hi, I\'m doing alright, thanks. I\'ve been having some issues with my heel.\n\n[doctor]: Oh, sorry to hear that. Can you tell me more about what\'s been going on?\n\n[patient]: Sure. I\'ve been having pain in my right heel for about 10 days now. It started while I was training for a triathlon.\n\n[doctor]: Hmm, that\'s interesting. Where exactly do you feel the pain?\n\n[patient]: It\'s mainly on the back of my heel, and it radiates up into my calf.\n\n[doctor]: Got it. How has the pain been affecting your daily activities?\n\n[patient]: It\'s been pretty bad. It gets worse when I work out and especially when I first get up in the morning.\n\n[doctor]: That sounds uncomfortable. Does the pain go away if you sit for a long time?\n\n[patient]: Yes, if I sit for a while, the pain goes away when I stand up.\n\n[doctor]: Okay. How often do you exercise and what kind of exercises do you do?\n\n[patient]: I run 10 kilometers in the morning, then at lunchtime, I go on my bike for about 45 minutes to an hour. In the evening, I swim for 30 minutes to an hour. I do this 5 days a week. On weekends, I alternate between a long run and a long bike ride. I take one day off from exercising.\n\n[doctor]: Wow, you\'re quite the athlete! Do you stretch after your workouts?\n\n[patient]: No, I don\'t stretch at all.\n\n[doctor]: Alright. Do you have any pain in your left leg?\n\n[patient]: No, just the right heel.\n\n[doctor]: Okay. Let\'s do a physical exam. Can you hop on the examination table, please?\n\n[patient]: Sure.\n\n[doctor]: Now, I\'m going to press down on the back of your heel. Does that hurt?\n\n[patient]: Yes, it does.\n\n[doctor]: Alright, now I\'m going to press down on the front of your heel. How does that feel?\n\n[patient]: It hurts a bit, but not as much as the back.\n\n[doctor]: Okay. Can you lift your heel up and point your toes towards your head? Any pain?\n\n[patient]: Yes, it hurts a bit.\n\n[doctor]: Alright. I\'m going to press down on your Achilles tendon. How does that feel?\n\n[patient]: It\'s very painful.\n\n[doctor]: I\'m going to do a calcaneal squeeze test now. Can you squeeze your heel?\n\n[patient]: Okay.\n\n[doctor]: I\'ll press down on your heel. Does it hurt?\n\n[patient]: Yes, it does.\n\n[doctor]: Alright, I\'m going to check the other side. Let\'s do the same thing on the left side. Any pain?\n\n[patient]: No, none.\n\n[doctor]: Okay. The Thompson test is negative on both sides. Based on your symptoms and the physical exam, it seems you have Achilles tendinitis in your right heel.\n\n[patient]: Achilles tendinitis? What does that mean?\n\n[doctor]: It means you have inflammation in the tendon connecting your calf muscle to your heel bone. This is likely due to overuse from your intense training.\n\n[patient]: Ah, I see. I guess that makes sense.\n\n[doctor]: Yes, it does. We\'ll treat you conservatively. I\'m going to recommend that you take at least a week off from your training to give your heel a chance to recover.\n\n[patient]: That\'s tough. I was really looking forward to my next triathlon.\n\n[doctor]: I understand. We\'ll get you back to training, but first, we need to give your heel a chance to heal. I\'ll also refer you to a physical therapist to help strengthen your right lower extremities.\n\n[patient]: Okay, I\'ll do that.\n\n[doctor]: Additionally, I\'ll prescribe you 3 tablets of ibuprofen every 6 hours for pain. If your pain doesn\'t improve with physical therapy, we\'ll need to consider an x-ray.\n\n[patient]: Alright, I\'ll follow your advice.\n\n[doctor]: Great. You\'ll need to follow up with me in 7 to 10 days to check on your progress. We\'ll reassess your symptoms and see how your heel is doing.\n\n[patient]: Alright, I\'ll make sure to come back in 7 to 10 days.\n\n[doctor]: Good. Do you have any questions for now?\n\n[patient]: No, I think that covers everything.\n\n[doctor]: Alright. We\'ll get you back on track. Take care and see you in a few weeks.\n\n[patient]: Thank you, I\'ll see you then. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Patient: Hi, hello.\n\nDoctor: Hi there. Hi.\n\nPatient: Hi. Hello.\n\nDoctor: Hello.\n\nDoctor: Hi.\n\nPatient: Hi.\n\nDoctor: Hi. Hi.\n\nDoctor: Hi.\n\nDoctor: Hi.\n\nDoctor: Hi.\n\nDoctor: Hi.\n\nDoctor: Hi.\n\nDoctor: Hi.\n\nDoctor: Hi.\n\nDoctor: Hi.\n\nDoctor: Hi.\n\nDoctor: Hi.\n\nDoctor: Hi.\n\nDoctor: Hi.\n\nDoctor: Hi.\n\nDoctor: Hi.\n\nDoctor: Hi.\n\nDoctor: Hi.\n\nDoctor: Hi.\n\nDoctor: Hi.\n\nDoctor: Hi.\n\nDoctor: Hi.\n\nDoctor: Hi.\n\nDoctor: Hi.\n\nDoctor: Hi.\n\