 fatigue. She states that she has been wearing her regular shoes.\n\nThe patient has type 2 diabetes with neuropathy. She states that her blood sugars are running better than they were; however, they are still in the 200\'s. Her last hemoglobin A1C was 10.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Reports fatigue.\nSkin: Reports right foot ulcer and drainage.\n\nVITALS\n\nNo fever noted. Vitals look good today.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: Examination of the right foot: There is a 1.0 cm x 2.0 cm wound on the dorsal aspect of the lateral right foot, just proximal to the 5th MTP joint. I do see some yellow slough that is present with minimal granulation tissue. No surrounding erythema or cellulitis. There is no evidence of fluid collection. I do not see necrosis. No odor. I do not appreciate any bony exposure. No pain to palpation.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right foot today reveals no evidence of osteomyelitis.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight diabetic foot ulcer.\n\nPLAN\n\nI have recommended that we obtain an ankle brachial index to determine her blood supply to see if we can heal the wound. We discussed doing a debridement in office today, which she agreed to. I have also recommended that the patient be placed in a surgical shoe to provide increased support. I have also prescribed the patient collagenase ointment to apply to her wound 1 time per day and cover it with a sterile dressing. I advised the patient that this should be continued until we see the ulcer decrease in size.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks to check on her progress.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor] Hello, how are you doing today?\n\n[patient] Hi, I\'m okay, but I\'ve been having some issues with my foot.\n\n[doctor] Oh, I\'m sorry to hear that. What seems to be the problem?\n\n[patient] My right foot has an open sore that won\'t heal.\n\n[doctor] I see. How long has this sore been there?\n\n[patient] It started about a year ago. I think it was just a blister at first, but I picked at it and it turned red.\n\n[doctor] Okay, and what happened after that?\n\n[patient] My primary care doctor prescribed me Keflex and it seemed to help, but it didn\'t really clear up.\n\n[doctor] Hmm, that\'s interesting. When did you stop taking the Keflex?\n\n[patient] About 5 months ago.\n\n[doctor] Alright. Have you noticed any pain with the sore?\n\n[patient] No, but I do feel less sensation in my foot.\n\n[doctor] That\'s concerning. Have you noticed any other symptoms?\n\n[patient] Yes, there\'s a lot of drainage and I feel really tired all the time.\n\n[doctor] Okay, I see. Have you been wearing your regular shoes?\n\n[patient] Yes, I haven\'t changed my routine.\n\n[doctor] Alright, let me go over your medical history. You have type 2 diabetes with neuropathy, correct?\n\n[patient] Yes, that\'s right.\n\n[doctor] How have your blood sugars been lately?\n\n[patient] They\'re better than they used to be, but they\'re still around 200.\n\n[doctor] And what was your last hemoglobin A1C?\n\n[patient] It was 10.\n\n[doctor] Okay, let\'s go over some other systems. How have you been feeling overall?\n\n[patient] I\'m tired all the time.\n\n[doctor] And how about your skin? Any other issues besides the sore on your foot?\n\n[patient] No, just the foot.\n\n[doctor] Alright, let me check your vitals. No fever, and your vitals look good today.\n\n[patient] Good.\n\n[doctor] Now, let\'s take a look at your foot. There is a 1 cm x 2 cm wound on the dorsal aspect of the lateral right foot, just proximal to the 5th MTP joint. I see some yellow slough present with minimal granulation tissue. There\'s no surrounding erythema or cellulitis, and no evidence of fluid collection. I don\'t see any necrosis, and there\'s no odor. I also don\'t see any bony exposure. No pain to palpation.\n\n[patient] Okay.\n\n[doctor] Let me order an ankle brachial index to check the blood supply to your foot. This will help us determine if we can heal the woun