ight radial artery cath site is clean, dry, and intact without hematoma. Radial artery pulses are palpable.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: Bilateral lower limb edema. Pitting 1+.\n\nRESULTS\n\nEKG is reviewed and demonstrates a normal sinus rhythm. Good R wave progression. Evolutionary changes that are anticipated after a STEMI.\n\nEchocardiogram is reviewed and demonstrates a reduced ejection fraction of 35%.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Coronary artery disease.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has a history of coronary artery disease that we will continue to monitor.\n- Medical Treatment: She will continue aspirin 81 mg daily and Brilinta 90 mg twice daily. She will continue Lipitor 80 mg a day and Toprol 50 mg daily. I am going to refer her to cardiac rehab for education and to gain confidence to resume exercising regularly.\n\n2. Newly reduced left ventricular dysfunction and moderate mitral regurgitation.\n- Medical Reasoning: A recent echocardiogram revealed a reduced ejection fraction of 35%. She was able to be treated in the cath lab in a timely manner, so I think that the muscle is just stunned. Given her history. it will improve over time.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient that she will recover since she is compliant with her medications.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue lisinopril 20 mg daily.\n\n3. Acute heart failure.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is retaining fluid.\n- Medical Treatment: I am going to prescribe Lasix 40 mg once a day. I want to add Aldactone 12.5 mg daily. I will order lab work. We will obtain a repeat echo in approximately 2 months.\n\n4. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: Blood pressure is well controlled. She will tolerate the Aldactone well.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged the patient to continue exercising and to be cognizant of her diet. I explained that there are healthier alternatives available to reduce the amount of preserved meats she consumes.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue lisinopril 20 mg daily. The patient received a referral to nutrition counseling to consider what other diet options might be available to her.\n\n5. Type 2 diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: Stable.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged the patient to continue monitoring her blood glucose levels.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue metformin as prescribed.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up as instructed.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor]: Hi there! How are you doing today?\n\n[patient]: Hi doctor, I\'m doing okay, thanks. A bit tired.\n\n[doctor]: Understandable. How has your recovery been since your recent hospitalization for the inferior STEMI?\n\n[patient]: It\'s been pretty good, I think. I\'ve been following the medication regimen you prescribed.\n\n[doctor]: Great to hear that! Let\'s review your medications. You\'re currently taking aspirin 81 mg daily, Brilinta 90 mg twice daily, Lipitor 80 mg daily, Toprol 50 mg daily, and lisinoprin 20 mg daily. Is that correct?\n\n[patient]: Yes, that\'s right.\n\n[doctor]: Excellent. Have you experienced any chest pain recently?\n\n[patient]: No, I haven\'t had any chest pain.\n\n[doctor]: Good. Have you noticed any shortness of breath?\n\n[patient]: No, I haven\'t had any shortness of breath.\n\n[doctor]: Any muscle or joint pain?\n\n[patient]: Yes, I\'ve had some swelling in my legs, both of them.\n\n[doctor]: Hmm, that\'s interesting. Your vital signs are all within normal limits. Let\'s do a quick physical exam. I\'ll start with your neck. Is there any jugular venous distension?\n\n[patient]: No, there isn\'t.\n\n[doctor]: Great. Let\'s listen to your lungs. They sound clear bilaterally.\n\n[patient]: Okay.\n\n[doctor]: Now, let\'s check your heart. I\'ll listen for any carotid bruits. I don\'t hear any. And I\'ll also listen to your heart sounds. I do hear a 3/6 systolic ejection murmur at the left base. This is expected after a STEMI. Your radial artery cath site looks good, no hematoma or issues there. Your radial pulses are palpable.\n\n[patient]: Okay.\n\n[doctor]: I\'ll now examine your legs. You mentioned swelling, and I can confirm that there is bilateral lower limb edema. It\'s mild, 1+.\n\n[patient]: Yes, I\'ve had that.\n\n[doctor]: I\'ve also reviewed your recent EKG, and it shows a normal sinus rhythm with good R wave progression. The changes we see are expected after a STEMI.\n\n[patient]: Alright.\n\n[doctor]: Your echocardiogram also shows a reduced ejection fraction of 35%. Given your history, I think this is likely due to stunned myocardium. With timely treatment, the muscle should recover over time.\n\n[patient]: That\'s good to hear.\n\n[doctor]: I\'ll continue you on aspirin 81 mg daily and Brilinta 90 mg twice daily. You\'ll also continue Lipitor 80 mg a day and Toprol 50 mg daily. I’m going to refer you to cardiac rehab for education and to gain confidence to resume exercising regularly.\n\n[patient]: Okay, I\'ll do that.\n\n[doctor]: Your echocardiogram also shows moderate mitral regurgitation. Given your history, we\'ll continue lisinoprin 20 mg daily.\n\n[patient]: Alright.\n\n[doctor]: You’re also experiencing acute heart failure, which is likely due to fluid retention. I’m going to prescribe Lasix 40 mg once a day and Aldactone 12.5 mg daily. We’ll also order some lab work and get a repeat echo in about two months.\n\n[patient]: Okay, I understand.\n\n[doctor]: Your blood pressure is well controlled, and you should tolerate the Aldactone well.\n\n[patient]: Alright.\n\n[doctor]: It’s also important to continue exercising and be mindful of your diet. There are healthier alternatives to reduce the amount of preserved meats you consume.\n\n[patient]: I’ll try to do that.\n\n[doctor]: I’ll also refer you to nutrition counseling to explore other diet options.\n\n[patient]: That sounds good.\n\n[doctor]: Your type 2 diabetes is stable, and you should continue monitoring your blood glucose levels.\n\n[patient]: Yes, I’ll keep monitoring.\n\n[doctor]: Any questions or concerns?\n\n[patient]: No, I think I understand everything.\n\n[doctor]: Great. You’ll follow up as instructed. Any issues or concerns in the meantime, feel free to reach out to me or my office.\n\n[patient]: I will. Thank you, doctor.\n\n[doctor]: You’re welcome. Take care and see you in a couple of months for that follow-up. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Patient: Hello?\n\nDoctor: Hello, hello.\n\nPatient: Hello?\n\nDoctor: Hi there. I\'m Doctor Smith. I\'m one of the doctors at your local medical centre. Can you hear me okay?\n\nPatient: Yes.\n\nDoctor: Okay. Can you hear me okay?\n\nPatient: Yes.\n\nDoctor: Okay. Hello, hi there. I\'m Doctor Smith. I\'m one of the doctors at your local medical centre. Can you hear me okay?\n\nPatient: Yes.\n\nDoctor: Okay. Hi there. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. Hi. 