 patient reports that she works on a keyboard all day.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nThe patient reports that she has been taking ibuprofen.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies sleep disturbance.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports pain in the 1st, 2nd, and 3rd digits on the right hand.\nNeurological: Reports numbness and tingling in the 1st, 2nd, and 3rd digits on the right hand.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: Examination of the right hand: Pain with flexion and extension of the wrist.. Decreased grip strength on the right compared to the left. Positive Tinel\'s sign.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight carpal tunnel syndrome.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing her symptoms and exam findings, I believe her symptoms are secondary to carpal tunnel syndrome. We discussed the nature of this condition, as well as her treatment options, in detail. I recommend we treat this conservatively with nighttime wrist splinting and ibuprofen, but I want to increase her dose to 600 mg 4 times daily. I\'m also going to order an EMG/Nerve conduction study for further evaluation. If this is positive, we can consider additional treatment with physical therapy or surgical intervention with carpal tunnel release.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor] Hello, how are you today?\n\n[patient] Hi, I\'m fine, thanks. I\'m here for my appointment.\n\n[doctor] Great, good to see you. What brings you in today?\n\n[patient] I\'ve been having some pain in my right hand.\n\n[doctor] Hmm, can you tell me more about the pain?\n\n[patient] It\'s been going on for about six months now. It started with numbness and tingling in my fingers, especially my thumb, index, and middle fingers. It\'s been constant for the past month.\n\n[doctor] Okay, so it\'s been constant for a month now. Have you noticed any other symptoms, like sleep disturbance?\n\n[patient] No, no sleep disturbance.\n\n[doctor] Alright. What do you usually do to manage the symptoms?\n\n[patient] I try to rest and take ibuprofen.\n\n[doctor] I see. How helpful has rest and ibuprofen been?\n\n[patient] They\'ve been helpful, but not entirely.\n\n[doctor] I understand. Do you work on a keyboard all day?\n\n[patient] Yes, I do. I use a mouse and keyboard all day.\n\n[doctor] That\'s helpful to know. Do you take any other medications besides ibuprofen?\n\n[patient] No, just ibuprofen.\n\n[doctor] Okay. Let\'s go over your medical history. Have you had any other medical issues recently?\n\n[patient] No, nothing else.\n\n[doctor] Have you noticed any other symptoms besides the numbness and tingling in your hand?\n\n[patient] No, just the pain.\n\n[doctor] Alright. Let\'s go through a few questions. Do you have any fever or chills?\n\n[patient] No, no fever or chills.\n\n[doctor] Any weight loss or weight gain?\n\n[patient] No, my weight is stable.\n\n[doctor] Any difficulty breathing?\n\n[patient] No, no difficulty breathing.\n\n[doctor] Okay. Any other medical issues?\n\n[patient] No, nothing else.\n\n[doctor] Alright, let\'s do a physical exam. Can you show me your right hand?\n\n[patient] Sure.\n\n[doctor] Let\'s start with the wrist. Can you flex and extend your wrist for me?\n\n[patient] Sure.\n\n[doctor] Okay. Any pain?\n\n[patient] Yes, it hurts.\n\n[doctor] I see. Let\'s check your grip strength. Can you squeeze my hand with your right hand?\n\n[patient] Okay.\n\n[doctor] Alright, I\'ll compare it to your left hand. Your right hand has decreased grip strength compared to your left. Let\'s check for Tinel\'s sign. I\'ll tap on the nerve at the wrist. This might be uncomfortable.\n\n[patient] Okay.\n\n[doctor] Alright, I\'ll tap on the nerve now. Can you feel any numbness or tingling?\n\n[patient] Yes, I feel it.\n\n[doctor] That\'s a positive Tinel\'s sign. Based on your symptoms and exam findings, I think you have carpal tunnel syndrome.\n\n[patient] What is that?\n\n[doctor] Carpal tunnel syndrome is a condition where the median nerve, which runs from the forearm into the hand through a narrow channel, gets compressed. It causes numbness, tingling, and pain in the hand and fingers. It\'s quite common in people who use their hands extensively, like yourself, who works on a keyboard all day.\n\n[patient] Okay, that makes sense.\n\n[doctor] We\'ll treat this conservatively for now. I recommend wearing a nighttime wrist splint to help reduce the pressure on the nerve. I\'ll also increase your ibuprofen dose to 600 mg, taken four times a day. This should help reduce the inflammation and pain.\n\n[patient] Okay, I can do that.\n\n[doctor] We\'ll also order an EMG/Nerve conduction study to confirm the diagnosis and rule out any other conditions. If the results come back positive, we can discuss further treatment options, such as physical therapy or surgical intervention with carpal tunnel release.\n\n[patient] Alright, I\'ll do that.\n\n[doctor] Great. We\'ll also monitor your symptoms and adjust the treatment plan as needed. Do you have any questions about the diagnosis or treatment?\n\n[patient] No, I think I understand. Thank you.\n\n[doctor] You\'re welcome. If you have any further questions or concerns, don\'t hesitate to reach out. We\'ll get you scheduled for the EMG/Nerve conduction study and follow up with you soon.\n\n[patient] Alright, thank you.\n\n[doctor] Take care, and we\'ll see you soon.\n\n[patient] Thanks, bye.\n\n[doctor] Bye. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Doctor: Hello. Hi, how are you today?\n\nPatient: I\'m good, thanks.\n\nDoctor: Great. Um, can you confirm your name for me?\n\nPatient: Yeah, it\'s Alexis Gutierrez.\n\nDoctor: OK, OK. Um, and your date of birth?\n\nPatient: Uh, it\'s, uh, 12th of February, 1990.\n\nDoctor: OK, OK. And your address?\n\nPatient: Uh, it\'s, uh, 12 High Street, uh, London, uh, SW1.\n\nDoctor: OK, OK. And your phone number?\n\nPatient: Uh, it\'s, uh, 0207 1234567.\n\nDoctor: OK, OK. And your email address?\n\nPatient: Uh, it\'s, uh, alexis.gutierrez@, uh, example.com.\n\nDoctor: OK, OK. And can you confirm your NHS number