[92mlitellm.acompletion(model='gpt-4o', api_base=None, messages=[{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a fair judge assistant assigned to deliver insightful feedback that compares individual performances, highlighting how each stands relative to others within the same cohort.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of two responses strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, choose a better response between Response A and Response B. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: "(write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (A or B)"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###Instruction:\n\nImagine you are a medical professional tasked with evaluating simulated doctor-patient conversations generated from summary notes. \nThese conversations should accurately reconstruct interactions based on the SOAP (Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan) format provided in the notes.\n\nHere is the medical note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight elbow pain.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\nGastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure: 150/90 mmHg\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\nGrade 2/6 systolic ejection murmur.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: Pain to palpation of the olecranon area of the posterior elbow. Mild pain with flexion and extension but otherwise normal range of motion. There is swelling about the elbow, as well as mild ecchymosis.\n\nHematology/Lymphatic/Immunology\n- Examination: Bruising noted over the right elbow.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right elbow was reviewed and demonstrates no evidence of fracture or bony abnormalities.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Right elbow contusion.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient sustained an injury to his elbow when his son struck him with a lacrosse stick. His x-ray and exam findings are consistent with an elbow contusion.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged the patient to avoid playing lacrosse for the next couple of weeks, just until his elbow heals. After that, he should be fine to continue playing normally.\n- Medical Treatment: I\'m going to prescribe him ibuprofen 800 mg twice daily to help with some of the pain and swelling. He should also ice the area in 20-minute intervals 3 times per day to further reduce his symptoms.\n\n2. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: This appears to be well-controlled with dietary modifications and lisinopril.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged him to keep up with his dietary modifications.\n- Medical Treatment: We will keep him on lisinopril 20 mg daily.\n\n3. Diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: This appears to be well controlled at this time.\n- Medical Treatment: We will keep him on metformin 500 mg and order a hemoglobin A1c to evaluate any need for medication adjustments.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor] Good morning, how are you doing today?\n\n[patient] Hi, I\'m doing alright, thanks. I\'ve been having some pain in my right elbow.\n\n[doctor] Ah, sorry to hear that. Can you tell me more about the pain? Where exactly is it and how long have you had it?\n\n[patient] It\'s in my right elbow, and it started a few days ago. My son and I were playing lacrosse and he accidentally hit me with the stick.\n\n[doctor] Oh no, that sounds like it could be quite painful. Have you noticed any other symptoms besides the pain?\n\n[patient] No, just the pain in my elbow.\n\n[doctor] Okay, have you experienced any chest pain or abdominal pain?\n\n[patient] No, none of that.\n\n[doctor] Good to know. Let\'s review your systems. How\'s your cardiovascular system been doing?\n\n[patient] It\'s been fine, no issues.\n\n[patient] And your gastrointestinal system?\n\n[patient] No issues there either.\n\n[patient] How about your musculoskeletal system? Any other pain or stiffness?\n\n[patient] Just the right elbow.\n\n[doctor] Alright, let\'s check your vital signs. Your blood pressure is 150/90 mmHg. Any change in your diet or stress levels recently?\n\n[patient] I\'ve been eating a bit more salty foods and I\'ve been under a bit of stress with work.\n\n[doctor] I see. Let\'s do a quick physical exam. I\'ll start with your respiratory system. Can you take a deep breath and cough for me?\n\n[patient] Sure.\n\n[doctor] Great. Your lungs sound clear bilaterally. Next, I\'ll check your cardiovascular system. I\'ll listen to your heart. Do you hear anything unusual?\n\n[patient] No, everything sounds normal.\n\n[doctor] Good. Now, let\'s check your musculoskeletal system. Can you move your right arm for me?\n\n[patient] Sure.\n\n[doctor] Can you flex and extend your elbow? Any pain or discomfort?\n\n[patient] Yes, there\'s pain when I do that.\n\n[doctor] And when I press on the back of your elbow, does that hurt?\n\n[patient] Yes, that hurts too.\n\n[doctor] I see. There\'s some swelling and ecchymosis around your elbow as well. Let me take a look at it. There\'s a bruise on your elbow. It looks like an injury. We\'ll do an X-ray to rule out any fractures or other bony abnormalities.\n\n[patient] Okay, sounds good.\n\n[doctor] We\'ll go ahead and order an X-ray of your right elbow. The results show no evidence of fracture or bony abnormalities. Based on your symptoms and the X-ray, it appears that you have a right elbow contusion.\n\n[patient] A contusion? What does that mean?\n\n[doctor] It means you have a bruise or injury to the soft tissues around your elbow. It\'s likely caused by the lacrosse stick hitting your elbow. The pain and swelling are common symptoms.\n\n[patient] Oh, I see. What do I need to do?\n\n[doctor] I\'ll prescribe ibuprofen 800 mg twice a day to help with the pain and swelling. You should also ice the area in 20-minute intervals three times a day to reduce the swelling and pain. Avoid playing lacrosse for the next couple of weeks until your elbow heals.\n\n[patient] Alright, that sounds good. What about my blood pressure? Is that related to the elbow?\n\n[doctor] No, your blood pressure is a separate issue. You\'ve been well-controlled with dietary modifications and lisinopril. Keep up with those dietary modifications and you should be fine.\n\n[patient] Okay, got it. And how about my diabetes?\n\n[doctor] Your diabetes appears to be well-controlled at this time. We\'ll keep you on metformin 500 mg. We\'ll also order a hemoglobin A1c to evaluate if any medication adjustments are needed.\n\n[patient] Alright, I understand. What else should I do?\n\n[doctor] Just follow the treatment plan I mentioned, including the ibuprofen and icing the elbow. If the pain gets worse or you notice any other symptoms, come back to see me. Otherwise, we\'ll follow up in a few weeks to check on your progress.\n\n[patient] Okay, I\'ll do that. Thank you.\n\n[doctor] You\'re welcome. Take care and feel better soon.\n\n[patient] Thanks, I will. Bye.\n\n[doctor] Bye. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Doctor: Hi there. How are you today?\n\nPatient: I\'m okay. I\'m a little sore.\n\nDoctor: Okay. How\'s your elbow feeling?\n\nPatient: It\'s sore.\n\nDoctor: Alright. And can you tell me more about what happened to your elbow?\n\nPatient: Um, I got hit with a lacrosse stick.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And can you tell me where exactly on your elbow you got hit?\n\nPatient: Um, on the back of it.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And how long ago did this happen?\n\nPatient: Um, I think it was yesterday.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And have you been having any pain or swelling in your elbow since then?\n\nPatient: Yeah, it\'s been getting progressively worse.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And have you been able to move your elbow around at all?\n\nPatient: Yeah, it\'s been kind of stiff.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And have you been able to straighten it out at all?\n\nPatient: Yeah, it\'s been kind of hard to straighten it out.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And have you been able to bend it at all?\n\nPatient: Yeah, it\'s been kind of hard to bend it.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And have you been having any numbness or tingling in your arm or hand?\n\nPatient: No, not really.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And have you been having any numbness or tingling in your fingers?\n\nPatient: No, not really.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And have you been having any difficulty moving your fingers at all?\n\nPatient: No, not really.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And have you been having any difficulty moving your hand at all?\n\nPatient: No, not really.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And have you been having any difficulty moving your shoulder at all?\n\nPatient: No, not really.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And have you been having any difficulty moving your neck at all?\n\nPatient: No, not really.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And have you been having any difficulty moving your head at all?\n\nPatient: No, not really.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And have you been having any difficulty moving your back at all?\n\nPatient: No, not really.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And have you been having any difficulty moving your legs at all?\n\nPatient: No, not really.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And have you been having any difficulty moving your hips at all?\n\nPatient: No, not really.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And have you been having any difficulty moving your feet at all?\n\nPatient: No, not really.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And have you been having any difficulty moving your toes at all?\n\nPatient: No, not really.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And have you been having any difficulty moving your knees at all?\n\nPatient: No, not really.\n\nDoctor: Okay. And have you been having any difficulty moving your ankles at 