taking ibuprofen for his pain, which has provided some relief. He does not recall any other previous injury to his right ankle.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports that he is very active and enjoys playing soccer. He currently plays for the community soccer league.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right ankle pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nVitals signs look good today.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: Mild edema\nNEURO: Normal sensation.\nMSK: Examination of the right ankle: Ecchymosis is present. There is swelling over the lateral malleolus. Tenderness to palpation anterolaterally in the soft tissue. No laxity on anterior drawer and inversion stress. No bony tenderness on palpation of the foot. Neurovascular intact. Capillary refill is less than 3 seconds with a strong dorsalis pedis pulse. Sensation is intact to light touch.\n\nRESULTS\n\n3 views of the right ankle were taken today. These reveal no evidence of fracture.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight ankle sprain.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. We discussed that his x-rays did not reveal any signs of a fracture and that he sustained a right ankle sprain. Treatment options were discussed. He was advised to keep his right lower extremity elevated, especially when seated. He will also continue to ice his right ankle. An Aircast was provided today to help stabilize his ankle. He is to also utilize crutches for the next 1 to 2 days followed by weight-bearing as tolerated. For pain control he can continue to take NSAIDs as needed. We discussed that he should see significant improvement in his symptoms over the next 2 weeks.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks to assess his progress and to determine his return to play date. He is advised to call the office should his symptoms change or worsen prior to his next appointment.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor] Hello, how are you doing today?\n\n[patient] Hi, I\'m okay. Thanks for seeing me.\n\n[doctor] Of course, I\'m Dr. Smith. What brings you in today?\n\n[patient] I\'ve been having some pain and swelling in my right ankle.\n\n[doctor] Hmm, okay. Can you tell me more about when the pain started?\n\n[patient] It started yesterday. I slipped on the ice while taking out the trash and fell. I couldn\'t move at first, and a friend had to help me up.\n\n[doctor] Oh no, that sounds like it must have been quite painful. How did you manage your ankle after the fall?\n\n[patient] I iced it and elevated it throughout the night. The pain has improved a bit, but I\'m still limping.\n\n[doctor] I see. Have you noticed any numbness in your right foot?\n\n[patient] No, I haven\'t.\n\n[doctor] Good. Have you had any other injuries to your right ankle in the past?\n\n[patient] No, this is the first time I\'ve ever had any issues with my right ankle.\n\n[doctor] Alright. How about your activity level? Do you do anything that might be related to this injury?\n\n[patient] Yes, I\'m very active. I play soccer in the community league.\n\n[doctor] Okay, that\'s good to know. Let\'s go over your medical history now. Have you noticed any other symptoms besides the pain and swelling?\n\n[patient] No, just the pain and swelling.\n\n[doctor] Alright. Any other medical conditions you\'re currently managing?\n\n[patient] No, I\'m generally healthy.\n\n[doctor] Great. Let\'s move on to your vital signs. Your blood pressure, heart rate, and oxygen saturation all look good today.\n\n[patient] Okay, that\'s good to hear.\n\n[doctor] Now, let\'s examine your ankle. I\'m going to gently feel your ankle to see if there\'s any tenderness or swelling. There is some mild edema present. How about any numbness or tingling?\n\n[patient] No, I don\'t feel any numbness.\n\n[doctor] Alright. I\'ll also check your ankle\'s range of motion. You mentioned you can bear more weight on your right ankle, but you\'re still limping?\n\n[patient] Yes, that\'s right.\n\n[doctor] Okay. I\'ll now perform some special tests to check for any instability. There\'s no laxity on anterior drawer and inversion stress. No bony tenderness when I palpate your foot. Your neurovascular exam is intact. Capillary refill is less than 3 seconds, and your dorsalis pedis pulse is strong.\n\n[patient] Alright, that sounds good.\n\n[doctor] I also took three views of your ankle. The x-rays didn\'t show any signs of a fracture.\n\n[patient] That\'s a relief.\n\n[doctor] Based on your symptoms and the exam findings, it appears that you\'ve sustained a right ankle sprain. We\'ll provide an Aircast to help stabilize your ankle.\n\n[patient] Okay, that sounds like it will help.\n\n[doctor] Yes, it will. You should keep your right lower extremity elevated, especially when seated. Continue to ice your ankle. You\'ll also need to use crutches for the next 1 to 2 days and then progress to weight-bearing as tolerated.\n\n[patient] Alright, I\'ll do that.\n\n[doctor] For pain control, you can continue to take NSAIDs as needed.\n\n[patient] Okay, I\'ll keep that in mind.\n\n[doctor] We\'ll also schedule a follow-up appointment in 2 weeks to assess your progress and determine your return to play date.\n\n[patient] That sounds good. I\'ll make sure to come back.\n\n[doctor] Great. If your symptoms change or worsen before then, please don\'t hesitate to call the office.\n\n[patient] I\'ll do that. Thank you, Doctor.\n\n[doctor] You\'re welcome. Take care and see you in 2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Doctor: Hi there. So can you start by telling me a little bit about your symptoms? You mentioned you have pain and swelling in your right ankle, is that right? \nPatient: Yeah. Yeah, I\'ve been having a lot of pain and swelling in my right ankle. I slipped on some ice and fell yesterday. \nDoctor: Okay. And you slipped on the ice, and fell on your right ankle. And you mentioned you couldn\'t m