 Examination of Abdomen: No hepatomegaly or splenomegaly.\n\nRESULTS\n\nRecent cholesterol level is reviewed and shows patient is elevated at 210.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Elevated cholesterol.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s previous tests are consistent with hypercholesterolemia.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed that the American Academy of Pediatrics recommends children between the ages of 9 to 11 years old and again between 17 to 20 years old be screened for their cholesterol. We also discussed that studies link high cholesterol to pre-mature heart attacks. I advised the patient that genetic testing can be done to see if the problem is genetic or a secondary cause. They were also advised to follow a balanced diet with a variety of foods that are low in saturated fat and sugars, as well as remain active.\n- Medical Treatment: Full cholesterol panel ordered. Hemoglobin A1c, CMP, thyroid study, and liver panel were also ordered. Genetic testing ordered. Referral to nutritionist provided.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow-up in 3 to 6 months after meeting the nutritionist for another cholesterol level.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor]: Hi there, how are you doing today?\n\n[patient]: Hi, I\'m doing alright. Thanks for seeing me.\n\n[doctor]: Great, thanks for coming in. I\'m Dr. Smith. What brings you here today?\n\n[patient]: My mom said I need to see you because my cholesterol is high.\n\n[doctor]: Ah, yes, that\'s correct. I see that you\'re here for a follow-up. How has your overall health been lately?\n\n[patient]: It\'s been pretty good, just been trying to take care of myself.\n\n[doctor]: That\'s great to hear. Do you smoke or use any tobacco products?\n\n[patient]: No, I don\'t smoke.\n\n[doctor]: Good. Do you have a history of smoking in your family?\n\n[patient]: Yes, my parents smoked when I lived with them.\n\n[doctor]: I see. Do you have any other hobbies or things you enjoy doing?\n\n[patient]: I like being outside and riding my bike on trails.\n\n[doctor]: That sounds like fun. Do you have any family history of heart disease or hypercholesterolemia?\n\n[patient]: My cousin died of a heart attack when they were in their 40\'s.\n\n[doctor]: I\'m sorry to hear that. But, are there any other family members with high cholesterol?\n\n[patient]: No, just my cousin.\n\n[doctor]: Okay, let\'s take a look at your vitals. Your blood pressure looks good. Now, let\'s do a physical exam.\n\n[doctor]: Can you lift your arms up for me? Good. Any swelling or pain in your neck?\n\n[patient]: No, nothing.\n\n[doctor]: Great. Let\'s listen to your lungs now. Can you take a deep breath for me? Your lungs sound clear bilaterally.\n\n[doctor]: Now, let\'s listen to your heart. I don\'t hear any murmurs, gallops, or rubs. Your abdomen feels normal too, no hepatomegaly or splenomegaly.\n\n[doctor]: Alright, let\'s review your recent cholesterol level. Your result is 210. This is a bit higher than we would like to see.\n\n[patient]: Oh no, that\'s not good.\n\n[doctor]: It\'s not ideal, but we can work with this. We\'ll discuss some lifestyle changes and medical treatment. The American Academy of Pediatrics recommends children like you be screened for cholesterol between the ages of 9 and 11, and again between 17 and 20. \n\n[patient]: Okay, I didn\'t know that.\n\n[doctor]: Yes, it\'s important to catch any issues early. Studies also show that high cholesterol can increase the risk of pre-mature heart attacks. We can also do genetic testing to see if this is a genetic issue or if there\'s a secondary cause.\n\n[patient]: That makes sense. What kind of lifestyle changes should I make?\n\n[doctor]: We recommend a balanced diet with a variety of foods that are low in saturated fats and sugars. It\'s also important to stay active, like you mentioned, by riding your bike.\