-d '{'model': 'gpt-4o', 'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a fair judge assistant assigned to deliver insightful feedback that compares individual performances, highlighting how each stands relative to others within the same cohort.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of two responses strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, choose a better response between Response A and Response B. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: "(write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (A or B)"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###Instruction:\n\nImagine you are a medical professional tasked with evaluating simulated doctor-patient conversations generated from summary notes. \nThese conversations should accurately reconstruct interactions based on the SOAP (Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan) format provided in the notes.\n\nHere is the medical note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBilateral knee pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nSophia Jackson is a pleasant 30-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of bilateral knee pain. She was referred by her primary care provider. The onset of her pain began 6 months ago. She denies any specific injury. The patient describes her pain as a deep, achy pain. She locates her pain to the posterior aspect of her patellae. The patient also reports “creaking” in her knees. She denies any swelling or redness in her knees. Her pain is aggravated by transitioning from a seated position to standing as well as ascending and descending stairs. Her pain is alleviated by rest. The patient has been icing her knees and wrapping her knees with an ACE bandage. She also takes ibuprofen or Aleve occasionally for pain. She states she tries to be as active as she can. She enjoys running outdoors. The patient adds that she would run 5 to 6 miles per day; however, she is unable to go 0.5 miles secondary to the pain. She denies any previous injuries to her knees.\n\nShe works from home.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of an upper extremity fracture when she was in 6th grade.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports bilateral knee pain and crepitus. Denies swelling or redness in the bilateral knees.\n\nVITALS\n\nAll vital signs are within the normal limits.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nGAIT: Normal, no deformity present.\nCV: Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs.\nNo edema\nNEURO: Patella and Achilles reflexes are symmetrical.\nMSK: Examination of the bilateral lower extremities: Abduction strength is 3/5. Remainder of muscle strength is 5/5. Examination of the bilateral knees: No overlying erythema, ecchymosis, or warmth of the skin appreciated. No effusion. Tenderness to palpation of the bilateral retinaculum. Positive patellar grind test. Knee pain with squatting. Negative Lachman test bilaterally. Negative anterior and posterior drawer tests bilaterally. Negative McMurray test bilaterally.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the bilateral knees were reviewed today. These demonstrate no evidence of any fractures or osteoarthritis.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nBilateral knee patellofemoral pain syndrome.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient regarding her current symptoms. I have explained to her that her symptoms are consistent with patellofemoral pain syndrome. We discussed treatment options and I have recommended that we begin with conservative treatment in the form of formal physical therapy to increase her lower extremity strength, mobility, and correct any incorrect running mechanics. I encouraged her to take running easy for now. She can continue taking ibuprofen or anti-inflammatories as needed for pain. All questions were answered.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me soon.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor] Hi there, Sophia. How are you doing today?\n\n[patient] Hi, doctor. I\'m doing okay, but my knees have been bothering me a lot lately.\n\n[doctor] I see. What\'s been going on with your knees?\n\n[patient] They\'ve been really sore and achy. It started about 6 months ago.\n\n[doctor] Hmm, okay. Do you remember any specific injury that might have caused the pain?\n\n[patient] No, I don\'t think so. It just started out of nowhere.\n\n[doctor] Alright. Where exactly do you feel the pain?\n\n[patient] It\'s on the back of my knees, on the patellae.\n\n[doctor] That sounds like it could be quite uncomfortable. Do you notice any swelling or redness in your knees?\n\n[patient] No, there\'s no swelling or redness.\n\n[doctor] That\'s good to know. How does the pain affect your daily activities?\n\n[patient] It\'s really bad. When I stand up from a seated position, it hurts. And going up and down stairs is really hard because of the pain.\n\n[doctor] I understand. Does resting help alleviate the pain?\n\n[patient] Yes, if I rest, it gets a bit better.\n\n[doctor] Okay. Have you tried any treatments for the pain?\n\n[patient] Yes, I\'ve been icing my knees and wrapping them with an ACE bandage. I also take ibuprofen or Aleve sometimes.\n\n[doctor] That\'s a good start. How about your physical activity? Do you try to stay active?\n\n[patient] I try to be as active as I can. I love running outdoors.\n\n[doctor] That\'s great. How far do you usually run?\n\n[patient] I usually run about 5 to 6 miles a day.\n\n[doctor] Wow, that\'s quite a distance! But you mentioned you\'re not able to go as far as you used to because of the pain. Can you tell me more about that?\n\n[patient] Yes, I can only go about 0.5 miles now because of the pain.\n\n[doctor] Hmm, that\'s quite a significant reduction. Have you ever had any previous injuries to your knees?\n\n[patient] No, nothing like that.\n\n[doctor] Alright. You mentioned you have a job that you do from home. Is that correct?\n\n[patient] Yes, that\'s right.\n\n[doctor] Okay. Have you had any other medical issues besides your knee pain?\n\n[patient] No, nothing else.\n\n[doctor] Great. Let\'s go over your medical history. You mentioned an upper extremity fracture when you were in 6th grade. Is that correct?\n\n[patient] Yes, I broke my arm in 6th grade.\n\n[doctor] Okay. Now, let\'s go over your systems. Do you have any other symptoms besides the knee pain?\n\n[patient] No, just the knee pain.\n\n[doctor] Alright. Any other issues with your joints or muscles?\n\n[patient] Just the knee pain.\n\n[doctor] Okay. Let\'s do a quick check of your vital signs. Your blood pressure, heart rate, and all other vital signs are normal.\n\n[patient] That\'s good to know.\n\n[doctor] Now, let\'s do a physical exam. I\'ll start by checking your gait. Do you have any difficulty walking or any noticeable deformities?\n\n[patient] No, I walk normally.\n\n[doctor] Great. Now, let\'s listen to your heart. Your heart sounds regular and normal. Do you have any edema or swelling in your legs?\n\n[patient] No, no swelling.\n\n[doctor] Okay. I\'ll check your neurologically. Your patella and Achilles reflexes are symmetrical, which is good.\n\n[patient] Okay.\n\n[doctor] Now, let\'s examine your lower extremities. Your muscle strength is good, except for the abduction strength which is 3/5. The rest of your muscle strength is 5/5.\n\n[patient] Alright.\n\n[doctor] I\'ll also check your knees. There\'s no overlying erythema, ecchymosis, or warmth of the skin. No effusion. I\'ll apply some pressure to your knees, and it\'s tender on the retinaculum. You have pain when I press on it.\n\n[patient] Yes, that hurts.\n\n[doctor] I\'ll do some other tests. Your patellar grind test is positive. You have pain when I do this test. Squatting also causes pain. But your Lachman test and anterior and posterior drawer tests are negative bilaterally. Your McMurray test is also negative.\n\n[patient] Okay.\n\n[doctor] We\'ll need to do some X-rays of your knees to rule out any fractures or osteoarthritis. The results show no evidence of any fractures or osteoarthritis.\n\n[patient] Alright.\n\n[doctor] Based on your symptoms and the examination findings, I think you have bilateral knee patellofemoral pain syndrome.\n\n[patient] What does that mean?\n\n[doctor] It means that the pain in your knees is related to the patellofemoral joint. We\'ll start with conservative treatment. I recommend you start formal physical therapy to increase your lower extremity strength, mobility, and correct any incorrect running mechanics. You should take it easy on your running for now.\n\n[patient] Okay.\n\n[doctor] You can continue taking ibuprofen or anti-inflammatories as needed for pain.\n\n[patient] Alright.\n\n[doctor] We\'ll follow up with you soon. Do you have any questions?\n\n[patient] No, I think I understand everything.\n\n[doctor] Great. Take care, Sophia. We\'ll see you soon.\n\n[patient] Thank you, doctor. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Patient: Hello?\nDoctor: Hello, Sophia.\nDoctor: Hi.\nPatient: Hi.\nDoctor: Hello, how can I help you today?\nPatient: Hi.\nDoctor: Hello, how can I help you today?\nPatient: Um, I\'m just having some knee pain. I\'m not sure what it is.\nDoctor: Okay. Okay. And how long have you had the pain for?\nPatient: Um, about six months now.\nDoctor: Okay. And how would you describe the pain?\nPatient: It\'s a deep, achy pain.\nDoctor: Okay. And where is the pain?\nPatient: Uh, in the back of my knees.\nDoctor: Okay. And is it worse when you move your knees?\nPatient: Yes.\nDoctor: Okay. And what kind of movement are you doing when it hurts?\nPatient: Um, like when I\'m standing up from a sitting positi