unable to take ibuprofen secondary to an ulcer.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: Examination of the left knee: Edema and effusion noted. Moderate ROM. Pain with flexion and extension of the knee. Negative Lachman\'s. Negative valgus and varus stress test.\n\nRESULTS\n\n4 views of the left knee were taken. These reveal no evidence of any fractures or dislocations. No other abnormalities are noted.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nLeft knee pain, possible MCL strain.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have discussed with the patient that her x-rays did not reveal any signs of a tear. I have recommended that we treat the patient conservatively. The plan is to place the patient in a knee immobilizer to provide increased support for the next week. I have also prescribed the patient tramadol 50 mg to treat her pain. She can take Tylenol as needed for breakthrough pain. I have also advised her to ice her left knee 3 to 4 times a day for 20 to 30 minutes at a time. I have advised her to refrain from running for the next 3 weeks. If her pain does not improve with the immobilizer, I will recommend obtaining an MRI.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor] Hello, how are you doing today?\n\n[patient] Hi, I\'m doing alright. Thanks for seeing me.\n\n[doctor] Not a problem at all. I\'m Dr. Smith. What brings you in today?\n\n[patient] I\'ve been having some pain in my left knee.\n\n[doctor] Hmm, okay. When did the pain start?\n\n[patient] It started about a week ago.\n\n[doctor] Oh, about a week ago. Can you tell me more about what happened?\n\n[patient] Yes, I was running around with my niece and I fell. I heard a pop when I landed. I think I twisted my knee.\n\n[doctor] I see. A pop is often a concerning sign. How has the pain been since then?\n\n[patient] It\'s been really bad. I\'ve been limping a lot.\n\n[doctor] Hmm, I see. Where exactly does the pain feel like it is located in your knee?\n\n[patient] It\'s on both the medial and lateral sides.\n\n[doctor] Okay, both sides. How would you rate your pain on a scale of 1 to 10?\n\n[patient] I\'d say it\'s a 4 out of 10. It\'s really throbbing.\n\n[doctor] Throbbing, that doesn\'t sound very pleasant. Have you been taking any medication for the pain?\n\n[patient] Yes, I\'ve been taking Tylenol, which helps a little bit, but it\'s not much. I also wrapped my knee with some kinesiology tape, which helps a bit too.\n\n[doctor] Okay, good to know. You\'re using some alternative methods to help with the pain. However, you mentioned you can\'t take ibuprofen because of an ulcer?\n\n[patient] Yes, that\'s correct. I have an ulcer and my doctor advised me not to take ibuprofen.\n\n[doctor] Alright, no ibuprofen for now. Let\'s do a physical exam. How does your knee feel when you move it?\n\n[patient] It hurts a lot when I bend or straighten it.\n\n[doctor] Okay. Let me take a closer look. (Examines the knee) I see some swelling and fluid around the knee. The range of motion is limited, and there\'s pain with flexion and extension. I\'m also going to check for any instability. (Performs Lachman\'s test and valgus and varus stress test) These tests are all negative.\n\n[patient] What does that mean?\n\n[doctor] The tests didn\'t indicate any significant instability or damage to the ligaments. Now, I\'m going to order some X-rays of your knee to rule out any fractures or dislocations.\n\n[patient] Okay, that sounds good.\n\n[doctor] We\'ll take four views of your left knee. I\'ll go ahead and order them. In the meantime, the X-rays didn\'t show any signs of a tear.\n\n[patient] That\'s good to hear.\n\n[doctor] Yes, it is. Based on the exam and the X-rays, it\'s possible that you may have a strain to the medial collateral ligament (MCL).\n\n[patient] What does that mean?\n\n[doctor] An MCL strain is a common injury. We\'ll treat you conservatively for now. I\'m going to prescribe tramadol 50 mg to help with the pain. You can take it as needed, 